10.1136/tobaccocontrol-2021-056528,54e43c3be7abe9d7,1,10.1001/jama.2016.14649,Association of e-cigarette vaping and progression to heavier patterns of cigarette smoking,"Leventhal AM, Stone MD, Andrabi N, et al",2016,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Leventhal AM, Stone MD, Andrabi N, et al"", ""year"": ""2016"", ""title"": ""Association of e-cigarette vaping and progression to heavier patterns of cigarette smoking"", ""doi"": ""10.1001/jama.2016.14649"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""In this paper, we present a replication study with new data using the same protocol as the cornerstone study by Leventhal et al.21 22"", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""For this study, we replicated an American cornerstone study21 22 on the association between e-cigarette use and tobacco smoking in the Netherlands and Flanders and found similar results."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Conclusion names both original citations as the replicated cornerstone work and says similar results were found. Discussion confirms both initiation and frequency associations.""}"
10.1002/aur.2496,fd1b2bbaa1b1b888,0,10.1007/s10803-009-0746-z,The autism diagnostic observation schedule—Toddler module: A new module of a standardized diagnostic measure for autism spectrum disorders.,"Luyster, R., Gotham, K., Guthrie, W., Coffing, M., Petrak, R., Pierce, K., Bishop, S., Esler, A., Hus, V., Oti, R., Richler, J., Risi, S., & Lord, C.",2009,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Luyster, R., Gotham, K., Guthrie, W., Coffing, M., Petrak, R., Pierce, K., Bishop, S., Esler, A., Hus, V., Oti, R., Richler, J., Risi, S., & Lord, C."", ""year"": ""2009"", ""title"": ""The autism diagnostic observation schedule—Toddler module: A new module of a standardized diagnostic measure for autism spectrum disorders."", ""doi"": ""10.1007/s10803-009-0746-z"", ""identified_by"": ""description"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""This study is the first replication of the ADOS-2 Toddler Module with a large independent sample. The optimal cut-off scores identified in the current study reflect the same results configured by the prior study."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The optimal cut-off scores identified in the current study reflect the same results configured by the prior study."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Explicit agreement of optimal cut-off scores with the prior study.""}"
10.1037/xlm0000874,ea74e3afbf0e5c8d,1,,The cocktail party phenomenon revisited: Attention and memory in the classic selective listening procedure of Cherry (1953).,"Wood NL, & Cowan N",1995,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Wood NL, & Cowan N"", ""year"": ""1995"", ""title"": ""The cocktail party phenomenon revisited: Attention and memory in the classic selective listening procedure of Cherry (1953)."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Wood and Cowan (1995)\nwere the first to examine shadowing errors in the relevant channel concurrently to the presentation of the name or shortly after. Consistent with\nMoray (1959)\n, 35 percent of the participants reported hearing their own name, and those who did were more likely to commit an error on a relevant to-be-shadowed word location."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""According to the retrospective questionnaire, 29 percent of the participants reported hearing their own name in the irrelevant message during a selective listening task. This is fairly comparable to the 33 percent from\nMoray (1959)\nand the 35 percent from\nWood and Cowan (1995)\n, and somewhat lower as the 43 percent from\nConway et al. (2001)\n, for lowest and highest quartiles averaged, and the 58 and 48 percent of younger adults in Experiments 1 and 4 of\nNaveh-Benjamin et al. (2014)\n."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The paper cites and its reference list names this entry; copied as printed despite potential bibliographic inconsistency.""}"
10.1037/xlm0000874,ea74e3afbf0e5c8d,2,,The cocktail party phenomenon revisited: The importance of working memory capacity.,"Conway AR, Cowan N, & Bunting MF",2001,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Conway AR, Cowan N, & Bunting MF"", ""year"": ""2001"", ""title"": ""The cocktail party phenomenon revisited: The importance of working memory capacity."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Replicating the finding of\nConway et al. (2001)\nthat individual differences in working memory span are related to errors in shadowing after the presentation of one’s own name, low span participants were more likely than high span participants to commit shadowing errors on a relevant to-be-shadowed word location. While this was true regardless of whether participants were classified based on their operation span scores or based on their running span scores, the effect was largest when participants were classified based on both scores combined. Thus it seems the effect is most reliably detected when more than one measure of working memory span is used."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The retrospective questionnaire proved insensitive to individual differences in working memory, probably because our lowest quartile (\nM\noperation span = 28.00, range 10-39) was well above the lowest quartile of Conway et al. (\nM\noperation span = 8.22, range 6-12). Taken together, our findings suggest that errors in shadowing caused by attention recruitment may occur in some individuals without rising to the point of conscious memory of the event."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Shadowing-error individual differences replicated, but retrospective conscious name detection was insensitive to span; the authors explicitly discuss both comparisons.""}"
10.1037/xlm0000874,ea74e3afbf0e5c8d,3,,Older adults do not notice their names: A new twist to a classic attention task.,"Naveh-Benjamin M, Kilb A, Maddox GB, Thomas J, Fine HC, Chen T, & Cowan N",2014,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Naveh-Benjamin M, Kilb A, Maddox GB, Thomas J, Fine HC, Chen T, & Cowan N"", ""year"": ""2014"", ""title"": ""Older adults do not notice their names: A new twist to a classic attention task."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": ""Experiments 1, 4"", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""As influential as it is, there are only a few direct replications of the phenomenon (\nConway, Cowan, & Bunting, 2001\n;\nNaveh-Benjamin et al., 2014\n;\nWood & Cowan, 1995\n)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""According to the retrospective questionnaire, 29 percent of the participants reported hearing their own name in the irrelevant message during a selective listening task. This is fairly comparable to the 33 percent from\nMoray (1959)\nand the 35 percent from\nWood and Cowan (1995)\n, and somewhat lower as the 43 percent from\nConway et al. (2001)\n, for lowest and highest quartiles averaged, and the 58 and 48 percent of younger adults in Experiments 1 and 4 of\nNaveh-Benjamin et al. (2014)\n."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Young-adult name-detection finding is retested and compared; the older-adult contrast is not tested.""}"
10.1037/xlm0000874,ea74e3afbf0e5c8d,4,,A semantic mismatch effect on serial recall: Evidence for interlexical processing of irrelevant speech.,"Röer JP, Bell R, Körner U, & Buchner A",2019,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Röer JP, Bell R, Körner U, & Buchner A"", ""year"": ""2019"", ""title"": ""A semantic mismatch effect on serial recall: Evidence for interlexical processing of irrelevant speech."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Moreover, recent findings suggest that words in the irrelevant auditory channel are not only processed at the level of their individual meaning, but also at the level of their match to the preceding semantic context (\nRöer, Bell, Körner, & Buchner, 2019\n;\nRöer, Buchner, & Bell, in press\n): Semantically unexpected endings (e.g.,\nToday was a beautiful day without a single cloud in the error\n) were more disruptive to serial recall than sentences with expected endings (e.g.,\nToday was a beautiful day without a single cloud in the sky\n). Unlike the disruptive effect of one’s name that of semantically unexpected words cannot be explained with occasional slips of attention to the irrelevant message, because the detection of a semantic expectation violation requires the integration of words into the sentence context."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In the present study, the presentation of unexpected words was not associated with shadowing errors in the relevant message, and only one participant reported that they noticed a semantically unexpected word, but could not remember which one, indicating that findings from cross-modal distraction tasks cannot be generalized to a situation in which the relevant and irrelevant sources of information are both presented auditorily."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Conceptual test of whether published semantic distraction generalizes to auditory selective listening; authors explicitly conclude it does not.""}"
10.1037/xlm0000874,ea74e3afbf0e5c8d,5,,Auditory distraction in short-term memory: Stable effects of semantic mismatches on serial recall.,"Röer JP, Buchner A, & Bell R",in press,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Röer JP, Buchner A, & Bell R"", ""year"": ""in press"", ""title"": ""Auditory distraction in short-term memory: Stable effects of semantic mismatches on serial recall."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Moreover, recent findings suggest that words in the irrelevant auditory channel are not only processed at the level of their individual meaning, but also at the level of their match to the preceding semantic context (\nRöer, Bell, Körner, & Buchner, 2019\n;\nRöer, Buchner, & Bell, in press\n): Semantically unexpected endings (e.g.,\nToday was a beautiful day without a single cloud in the error\n) were more disruptive to serial recall than sentences with expected endings (e.g.,\nToday was a beautiful day without a single cloud in the sky\n). Unlike the disruptive effect of one’s name that of semantically unexpected words cannot be explained with occasional slips of attention to the irrelevant message, because the detection of a semantic expectation violation requires the integration of words into the sentence context."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In the present study, the presentation of unexpected words was not associated with shadowing errors in the relevant message, and only one participant reported that they noticed a semantically unexpected word, but could not remember which one, indicating that findings from cross-modal distraction tasks cannot be generalized to a situation in which the relevant and irrelevant sources of information are both presented auditorily."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Second primary semantic-mismatch paper named jointly in the finding tested for generalization; year only supplied as in press.""}"
10.1177/08884064211001453,0091161cad153f3e,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,"{""target_index"": 0, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""obvious"", ""verdict"": ""undeterminable"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""Given the critical importance of discrete instructional practices in special education, teacher candidates must be prepared to implement them upon entering the classroom. In preservice teacher education programs, field placements and clinical experiences rarely provide enough opportunities for preservice teachers to gain the proficiency needed to provide effective instruction. In this study, a randomized control research design was used to investigate the effects of a mixed-reality simulation experience compared with traditional classroom practice in the implementation of a system of least prompts. Results suggest that mixed-reality simulation with additional coaching supports significantly improved preservice teachers’ implementation of the prompting sequence. Social validity data collected offer insights into the use of mixed-reality simulation in practice with preservice teachers. Limitations and suggestions for future work are discussed."", ""location"": ""Complete available primary abstract; independent original-identity validation where specified"", ""reason"": ""Actual primary evidence-access/identity limit: complete available abstract describes a new mixed-reality/coaching prompting experiment but names no original authors, year or reference. The supplied Vince-Garland2015 title cannot establish an original-specific relationship merely by topical similarity or being the only offered counterpart. The full follow-up acquisition record is not_found. Two independent Argon replies also described this primary limitation, but their whole bundle remains unvalidated; parent independently read the complete abstract. Retain unresolved candidate without listing any reference as missing. NEW parent primary-source disposition; no rejected provider quote was repaired, no partial model reply was promoted and no attempt budget reset.""}","{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Mixed-Reality Simulation With Preservice Teacher Candidates: A Conceptual Replication"", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""outcome_quote"": """", ""outcome_quote_section"": """", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Abstract reports improved performance but does not identify or state the original finding; comparison cannot be established. Unidentified target retained rather than guessing from references to other simulation work.""}"
10.1101/lm.053631.122,4f9e3c61cd8b49b3,1,10.1007/BF00308685,Motivated memory: dissociation between performance data and subjective reports,Nilsson L-G.,1987,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Nilsson L-G."", ""year"": ""1987"", ""title"": ""Motivated memory: dissociation between performance data and subjective reports"", ""doi"": ""10.1007/BF00308685"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Replicating our own and other previous studies (Nilsson 1987; Ngaosuvan and Mantila 2005; experiment 1 in Stefanidi et al. 2018; Talmi et al. 2021), reward did not increase recall in pure lists."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Replicating our own and other previous studies (Nilsson 1987; Ngaosuvan and Mantila 2005; experiment 1 in Stefanidi et al. 2018; Talmi et al. 2021), reward did not increase recall in pure lists."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors explicitly frame the absence of a pure-list reward benefit as replicating this paper.""}"
