10.1017/s0003055422000946,a0c4032c0bd9530a,0,,Self-Censorship of Regime Support in Authoritarian States: Evidence from List Experiments in China.,"Robinson, Darrel, and Tannenberg, Marcus",2019,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Robinson, Darrel, and Tannenberg, Marcus"", ""year"": ""2019"", ""title"": ""Self-Censorship of Regime Support in Authoritarian States: Evidence from List Experiments in China."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""description"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We replicated previous list experiment results confirming that a majority trusts the central government despite overreports."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We replicated previous list experiment results confirming that a majority trusts the central government despite overreports."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The abstract explicitly says previous list-experiment findings were replicated. This supports the described target; novel local-government and term-limit studies are not additional targets.""}"
10.1027/1864-9335/a000188,fe0e494e6758464b,1,,,Gong and Medin,2012,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Gong and Medin"", ""year"": ""2012"", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Eyal, Liberman, and Trope (2008) established that people judged moral transgressions more harshly and virtuous acts more positively when the acts were psychologically distant than close. In a series of conceptual and direct replications, Gong and Medin (2012) came to the opposite conclusion. Attempting to resolve these inconsistencies, we conducted four high-powered replication studies in which we varied temporal distance (Studies 1 and 3), social distance (Study 2) or construal level (Study 4), and registered their impact on moral judgment."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Eyal, Liberman, and Trope (2008) established that people judged moral transgressions more harshly and virtuous acts more positively when the acts were psychologically distant than close. In a series of conceptual and direct replications, Gong and Medin (2012) came to the opposite conclusion. Attempting to resolve these inconsistencies, we conducted four high-powered replication studies in which we varied temporal distance (Studies 1 and 3), social distance (Study 2) or construal level (Study 4), and registered their impact on moral judgment. We found no systematic effect of temporal distance, the effect of social distance consistent with Eyal et al., and the reversed effect of direct construal level manipulation, consistent with Gong and Medin. Possible explanations for the incompatible results are discussed."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The construal effect agrees with Gong and Medin, while the social distance result agrees with their stated opposite Eyal finding. The abstract does not map original study numbers.""}"
10.1155/2013/639702,0ab1768ae9c6e310,1,,Object individuation in young infants: further evidence with an event-monitoring paradigm,"Wilcox, T., and Baillargeon, R.",1998,full_text,same_author_year_siblings,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Wilcox, T., and Baillargeon, R."", ""year"": ""1998"", ""title"": ""Object individuation in young infants: further evidence with an event-monitoring paradigm"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The direct Narrow-Control test                  Our answer to this question is twofold. First, this phe-\nthat we employed has been used in studies by Wilcox and                nomenon is actually not rare at all in the infant research\nher collaborators as well. For example, in their followup              literature. For example, when two distinct and different-\nto the reference study, Wilcox and Baillargeon [5] obtained"", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Resembling the results obtained through the\nSLTP, the eye tracking data failed to provide evidence that the                               15.00\ninfants used featural information to discriminate between the\n\n\n\n\n                                                                     Mean fixation time (s)\nobjects involved."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The paper implements the direct narrow-control comparison previously used in reference [5] and explicitly contrasts its null discrimination comparison with that paper’s shorter ball-ball than ball-box looking times. This is a retested finding with a new sample, despite the age difference. The evidence quote preserves the interleaved text exactly as supplied, without joining columns or repairing text.""}"
