10.1080/22243534.2015.11828344,1c44b19bafd0d2c9,0,,,Hofstede,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,"{""corrected_value"": null, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""1c44b19bafd0d2c9"", ""location"": ""Abstract (line 5)"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""In the present study the cultural dimensions of the well known Dutch researcher Geert Hofstede were tested on a sample of international business students. The sample consisted of 1 033 students from the Netherlands, Germany, China, South Africa and Qatar. The findings of the present research contest many of Hofstede's original findings. The dimensions power distance and long-term orientation show significant differences with Hofstede's original country values."", ""reason"": ""No prior adjudication or state file exists for this work, and Luna's prior spliced quote is replaced with an intact contiguous passage. The available primary source consists only of the title, author list, and abstract, which reports testing Geert Hofstede's cultural dimensions on 1,033 international business students from five countries and comparing them against Hofstede's original country values. Because the abstract gives no publication year, title, or reference list for the Hofstede work(s) supplying those original country values, the evidence cannot resolve which specific Hofstede publication is retested or whether shipped row 231 (Hofstede, 1999, 'Culture's consequences: International differences in work-related values') already represents the target work."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0008_1c44b19bafd0d2c9"", ""target_index"": 0, ""verdict"": ""undeterminable""}","{""authors"": ""Hofstede"", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""In the present study the cultural dimensions of the well known Dutch researcher Geert Hofstede were tested on a sample of international business students."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The findings of the present research contest many of Hofstede's original findings. The dimensions power distance and long-term orientation show significant differences with Hofstede's original country values. The dimensions individualism, masculinity and uncertainty avoidance show significant differences for half of the countries."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Abstract contests many original findings, with discrepancies on two dimensions and only half the countries on three dimensions; coded as partial agreement/disagreement with uncertainty because no full results are available. No reference list supplied, so cannot resolve whether the underlying original findings belong to one or multiple Hofstede publications.""}"
10.1002/jocb.350,0f58697d4f41df92,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,"{""corrected_value"": null, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""0f58697d4f41df92"", ""location"": ""Abstract (line 3)"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""We could also replicate a positive relationship of mental speed with speeded divergent thinking (r = .21) and mental speed with unspeeded divergent thinking (r = .25)."", ""reason"": ""No prior adjudication or state files exist for this request. The supplied source text consists solely of an abstract (evidence_level: abstract) containing no citations or references. Although the abstract explicitly states that the authors replicated a positive relationship of mental speed with speeded and unspeeded divergent thinking, the abstract alone does not identify the original work(s) and cannot establish whether the retested finding is exclusively shipped row 246 (Preckel, Wermer, & Spinath, 2011, 10.1016/j.intell.2011.06.007) or an omitted original paper."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0003_0f58697d4f41df92"", ""target_index"": 0, ""verdict"": ""undeterminable""}","{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We could also replicate a positive relationship of mental speed with speeded divergent thinking (r = .21) and mental speed with unspeeded divergent thinking (r = .25)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We could also replicate a positive relationship of mental speed with speeded divergent thinking (r = .21) and mental speed with unspeeded divergent thinking (r = .25)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The abstract explicitly states the positive relationships replicated, but does not identify the earlier paper(s); target identity and number remain uncertain.""}"
10.1016/j.system.2020.102377,f133a7e9cc8706dc,0,,,Henry and Cliffordson,2017,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Henry and Cliffordson"", ""year"": ""2017"", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Results replicated those of Henry and Cliffordson (2017) for initial hypotheses related to motivational selves; current and ideal selves were statistically significant predictors of effort, in line with prior studies in the Chinese context."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Results replicated those of Henry and Cliffordson (2017) for initial hypotheses related to motivational selves; current and ideal selves were statistically significant predictors of effort, in line with prior studies in the Chinese context. However, results among this sample supported discrete constructs for ideal L2 and current L2 self and provide evidence of a separable sub-component of self-authenticity, digital self-authenticity."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Initial hypotheses replicated but the abstract frames construct results as contrasting with them. Publisher conclusion preview also explicitly refers to discrepancies with Henry and Cliffordson. Full results unavailable; mixed is uncertain.""}"
10.1017/s0033291720001919,7252b65650fbd13d,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""This study aimed to replicate research behind Climate Schools: Alcohol and Cannabis course among a large cohort of adolescents."", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""outcome_quote"": """", ""outcome_quote_section"": """", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Abstract explicitly reports replicating alcohol effects but names no original paper or uniquely bounded set of papers. It does not establish which findings from which original were re-tested, so a per-original verdict cannot be assigned. An unresolved target placeholder represents missing identification, not a claim that only one original exists.""}"
10.1108/ijpdlm-04-2021-0121,a15282356e0c998b,1,,Power and relationship commitment: Their impact on marketing channel member performance.,"Brown, J., Lusch, R. & Nicholson, C. Y.",1995,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Brown, J., Lusch, R. & Nicholson, C. Y."", ""year"": ""1995"", ""title"": ""Power and relationship commitment: Their impact on marketing channel member performance."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We find that the impact of non-mediated power  on normative relationship commitment and \n\nthat  of  mediated  power  on  instrumental  relationship  commitment  in  the  United  States  are \n\nconsistent with the findings of Brown et al. (1995). Moreover, the results show that expert and \n\nlegitimate power are negatively associated with whereas referent power is positively associated \n\nwith  instrumental  relationship  commitment,  which  provides  a  possible  explanation  of  the \n\nfinding  of  the  insignificant  effect  of  non-mediated  power  on  instrumental  relationship \n\ncommitment  (Brown  et  al.,  1995).  The  result  reveals  that  different  bases  of  non-mediated \n\npower  may  have  conflicting  effects  on  instrumental  relationship  commitment  and  hence \n\nresearchers need to consider the individual effects of different bases of non-mediated power. \n\nMoreover, we find that reward, coercive and legal legitimate power do not significantly affect \n\nnormative relationship  commitment,  whereas Brown et  al. (1995) find that mediated power \n\nsignificantly reduces normative relationship commitment. Brown et al. (1995) also investigate \n\nthe moderating effect of symmetry of power and find that a supplier’s use of mediated power \n\ndoes  not  significantly  affect  normative  relational  commitment  when  the  supplier  is  more \n\npowerful. We focus on a manufacturer’s major customers and hence the customers usually are \n\nmore powerful. Thus, the result supports Brown et al.’s (1995) finding on the moderating effect \n\nof symmetry of power. The finding suggests that researchers should consider the moderating \n\neffect of symmetry of power when investigating the impacts of mediated power."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We find that the impact of non-mediated power  on normative relationship commitment and \n\nthat  of  mediated  power  on  instrumental  relationship  commitment  in  the  United  States  are \n\nconsistent with the findings of Brown et al. (1995). Moreover, the results show that expert and \n\nlegitimate power are negatively associated with whereas referent power is positively associated \n\nwith  instrumental  relationship  commitment,  which  provides  a  possible  explanation  of  the \n\nfinding  of  the  insignificant  effect  of  non-mediated  power  on  instrumental  relationship \n\ncommitment  (Brown  et  al.,  1995).  The  result  reveals  that  different  bases  of  non-mediated \n\npower  may  have  conflicting  effects  on  instrumental  relationship  commitment  and  hence \n\nresearchers need to consider the individual effects of different bases of non-mediated power. \n\nMoreover, we find that reward, coercive and legal legitimate power do not significantly affect \n\nnormative relationship  commitment,  whereas Brown et  al. (1995) find that mediated power \n\nsignificantly reduces normative relationship commitment."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The US sample directly tests the power-commitment findings of Brown et al.; the dedicated comparison states both agreements and a contrast for mediated power and normative commitment. The later moderation comparison supports one part, not an overall verdict.""}"
10.1177/25152459241267902,5f752faa16b30af3,1,10.1016/j.concog.2016.01.004,"Vision, knowledge, and assertion.","Turri, J.",2016,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Turri, J."", ""year"": ""2016"", ""title"": ""Vision, knowledge, and assertion."", ""doi"": ""10.1016/j.concog.2016.01.004"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": ""Experiment 7"", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Next, we explored whether attributions of outcomes to luck versus ability influence knowledge attributions, as suggested by prior research (Turri, 2016, 2017)."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Thus, unlike in previous research (Turri, 2016, 2017), luck attributions did not decrease the likelihood of participants demonstrating Gettier intuitions."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Explicit comparisons to the stated original finding determine the verdict.""}"
