10.32872/cpe.v2i4.2593,8ac348d62cc5f33c,1,10.1080/02699931.2016.1154817,The beneficial and detrimental effects of major depression on intuitive decision-making.,"Remmers C., Topolinski S., Buxton A., Dietrich D. E., Michalak J.",2017,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Remmers C., Topolinski S., Buxton A., Dietrich D. E., Michalak J."", ""year"": ""2017"", ""title"": ""The beneficial and detrimental effects of major depression on intuitive decision-making."", ""doi"": ""10.1080/02699931.2016.1154817"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The first hypothesis (H1) refers to the replicability of recently found intuition deficits in depression ( Remmers et al., 2015 ; Remmers et al., 2017 )."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Overall the current study did not replicate findings from previous research regarding intuitive semantic coherence detection deficits in depression."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The overall deficit replication verdict covers the two originals explicitly named for H1.""}"
10.32872/cpe.v2i4.2593,8ac348d62cc5f33c,2,10.1037/a0014678,The architecture of intuition: Fluency and affect determine intuitive judgments of semantic and visual coherence and judgments of grammaticality in artificial grammar learning.,"Topolinski S., Strack F.",2009,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Topolinski S., Strack F."", ""year"": ""2009"", ""title"": ""The architecture of intuition: Fluency and affect determine intuitive judgments of semantic and visual coherence and judgments of grammaticality in artificial grammar learning."", ""doi"": ""10.1037/a0014678"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": ""Experiment 8 (valence); fluency experiment unspecified"", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Building up on basic research ( Topolinski & Strack, 2009a ; Experiment 8) showing that healthy subjects are more likely to judge triads to be coherent that consist of positive as compared to negative words, we expected that this effect would also emerge for depressed patients."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Results did not reveal any effect of our fluency manipulation, and thus our hypotheses regarding fluency (H2) were not supported. As such, the current study could not replicate previous results that bolstered the fluency model proposed by Topolinski and Strack (2009a , 2009b )."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Fluency comparison fails, while positive-valence comparison agrees. The fluency passage explicitly contrasts prior results. Additional agreement passage: The results further showed that positive (vs. negative) valence triggered coherence judgments (H3a) and that this effect was – in contrast to our hypothesis (H3b) – not moderated by depression.""}"
10.32872/cpe.v2i4.2593,8ac348d62cc5f33c,3,10.1080/02699930802420745,The analysis of intuition: Processing fluency and affect in judgements of semantic coherence.,"Topolinski S., Strack F.",2009,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Topolinski S., Strack F."", ""year"": ""2009"", ""title"": ""The analysis of intuition: Processing fluency and affect in judgements of semantic coherence."", ""doi"": ""10.1080/02699930802420745"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Results did not reveal any effect of our fluency manipulation, and thus our hypotheses regarding fluency (H2) were not supported. As such, the current study could not replicate previous results that bolstered the fluency model proposed by Topolinski and Strack (2009a , 2009b )."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Results did not reveal any effect of our fluency manipulation, and thus our hypotheses regarding fluency (H2) were not supported. As such, the current study could not replicate previous results that bolstered the fluency model proposed by Topolinski and Strack (2009a , 2009b )."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors explicitly include this paper in the previous fluency results they could not replicate. No separately attributable positive-valence retest is stated for this original.""}"
