10.1515/iral-2012-0001,7399924f3ac7dc01,2,,Constructions as categories of language: The role of order on construction learning.,"Goldberg, Adele E., Casenhiser, Devin & White, Tiffani.",2007,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Goldberg, Adele E., Casenhiser, Devin & White, Tiffani."", ""year"": ""2007"", ""title"": ""Constructions as categories of language: The role of order on construction learning."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Goldberg et al. (2007) showed that initially providing skewed input facilitated rapid construction generalization. The present study investigates this effect for the position of verbs with relative high token frequency."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""There was a significant main effect for order of frequent verbs in the comprehension of the APPEARANCE construction. Figure 1 shows that the (+forward order) group outperformed the (−forward skewing) group. No significant effects were found in the guided production of the APPEARANCE construction and the comprehension of the Samoan ergative construction."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors present an agreeing comprehension effect and explicitly absent effects in the other tasks; no overall verdict supersedes this comparison.""}"
