10.1080/00221546.2019.1650582,47a8be34f8916560,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,"{""corrected_value"": null, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""47a8be34f8916560"", ""location"": ""Abstract (line 5) of 47a8be34f8916560.txt"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""Though low-cost value affirmation interventions have been shown to reduce achievement gaps in some classroom settings, these findings have not been consistently replicated. In this study, we test the efficacy of this intervention among a new sample of students enrolled in a community college in the Midwest."", ""reason"": ""The primary source file (47a8be34f8916560.txt, Baker, Skinner, & Redding, DOI 10.1080/00221546.2019.1650582) contains only the title and a 6-sentence abstract with no in-text citations, methods/results comparisons, or reference list. While the abstract states that low-cost value affirmation interventions have been shown to reduce achievement gaps in some classroom settings and tests the efficacy of this intervention in a new community college sample, it does not name any specific original study. Consequently, it cannot be verified from the available source text which specific published work is empirically retested or whether the unnamed candidate in target_index 0 is already represented by shipped row 302 (Cohen, Garcia, Apfel, & Master, 2006, Science, DOI 10.1126/science.1128317) versus a distinct omitted original study. Prior adjudication and state files were checked and do not exist."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0033_47a8be34f8916560"", ""target_index"": 0, ""verdict"": ""undeterminable""}","{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Though low-cost value affirmation interventions have been shown to reduce achievement gaps in some classroom settings, these findings have not been consistently replicated. In this study, we test the efficacy of this intervention among a new sample of students enrolled in a community college in the Midwest."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Overall, we find little evidence of a positive effect of this one-time affirmation of social identity."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Overall verdict indicates little evidence for the intervention effect. Abstract does not identify the original paper; no citation guessed.""}"
10.1371/journal.pone.0220419,d54c6bf1f5190f00,0,,"Interfering with theories of sleep and memory: sleep, declarative memory, and associative interference.","Ellenbogen JM, Hulbert JC, Stickgold R, et al.",2006,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Ellenbogen JM, Hulbert JC, Stickgold R, et al."", ""year"": ""2006"", ""title"": ""Interfering with theories of sleep and memory: sleep, declarative memory, and associative interference."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""However, two studies by Ellenbogen, et al. [\n8\n,\n9\n] have been by far the most cited and most influential evidence for sleep’s proposed role in protecting against interference. These studies form the basis for the present replication."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""However, we did not replicate Ellenbogen et al.’s observation of a large effect of sleep in protecting memories against interference learning. Instead, we observed that the Sleep and Wake groups both experienced a significant and very similar retroactive interference effect."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The central protective effect failed; title and abstract give the overall failure verdict.""}"
10.1371/journal.pone.0220419,d54c6bf1f5190f00,2,,Scopolamine does not impact declarative and motor memory consolidation across a night of sleep or a day of wakefulness.,"Tucker MA, Taylor K, Merchant R, et al.",2018,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Tucker MA, Taylor K, Merchant R, et al."", ""year"": ""2018"", ""title"": ""Scopolamine does not impact declarative and motor memory consolidation across a night of sleep or a day of wakefulness."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We have also shown that increased intrinsic motivation (the desire to do well even in the absence of extrinsic rewards), measured by responses to items from the Intrinsic Motivation Inventory [\n27\n] used in the present study, correlated with better acquisition of visual paired associates (picture-object pairs) at training, and also with change in performance from training to retest (retention) [\n26\n]."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In the present study, we observed a similar positive correlation between intrinsic motivation and training and retest performance. However, motivation did not correlate with memory consolidation (change in performance from training to retest), nor did intrinsic motivation favor performance in the sleep (v. wake) participants."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The separately hypothesized re-test of a published intrinsic-motivation finding agrees on acquisition but explicitly differs on consolidation.""}"
10.1016/s0163-6383(83)80012-5,592d372d41a77ec8,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""The purpose of the present research was to replicate two experiments in which neonatal imitation was reported."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The results of both replications failed to yield evidence of neonatal imitation and thus raise questions about the neonate’s capacity to imitate."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The abstract gives an explicit verdict covering both attempts, but names neither original. This unnamed entry records the unresolved target relationship; the number of distinct original papers cannot be established from abstract evidence.""}"
10.2307/2586310,b91a64486d8bd684,1,,,"MacKuen, Erikson, and Stimson",1992,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""undeterminable"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""MacKuen, Erikson, and Stimson (1989, 1992) argue that the aggregate distribution of party identification, or macropartisanship, shifts significantly over short periods in response to changes in presidential popularity and consumer sentiment. Their results are based on a subset of Gallup surveys available from 1953 to 1988 and only those CBS/New York Timessurveys conducted during the Reagan administration. We replicate this analysis using a more extensive inventory of Gallup and CBS/New York Timesdata and find considerably less evidence of partisan fluctuation. The amount of partisan change caused by short-term movements in consumer sentiment and presidential popularity is found to be two to three times smaller than initially reported by MacKuen et al. (1989)."", ""location"": ""abstract"", ""reason"": ""The abstract jointly cites MacKuen, Erikson, and Stimson (1989, 1992) before referring to '[t]heir results' and stating '[w]e replicate this analysis', while explicitly quantifying the comparison only against MacKuen et al. (1989). Because only the abstract is available and the full text and reference list are unseen, the supplied source neither establishes that the 1992 study is independently re-tested nor proves its exclusion from the full-text replication.""}","{""authors"": ""MacKuen, Erikson, and Stimson"", ""year"": ""1992"", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""MacKuen, Erikson, and Stimson (1989, 1992) argue that the aggregate distribution of party identification, or macropartisanship, shifts significantly over short periods in response to changes in presidential popularity and consumer sentiment. We replicate this analysis using a more extensive inventory of Gallup and CBS/New York Timesdata and find considerably less evidence of partisan fluctuation."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The amount of partisan change caused by short-term movements in consumer sentiment and presidential popularity is found to be two to three times smaller than initially reported by MacKuen et al. (1989). Our results indicate that macropartisanship adjusts to short-term shocks in a limited and gradual fashion, consistent with traditional views of partisan realignment."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The abstract describes both cited originals as reporting responsiveness to these inputs and reports responsiveness in the same direction but smaller. Reduced magnitude alone does not imply mixed. Full-text verdict and reference entries unavailable.""}"
