10.1080/15475441.2014.979387,fa44ae0be27e328b,1,10.1016/j.cognition.2013.02.011,The acquisition of abstract words by young infants.,Bergelson E; Swingley D,2013b,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Bergelson E; Swingley D"", ""year"": ""2013b"", ""title"": ""The acquisition of abstract words by young infants."", ""doi"": ""10.1016/j.cognition.2013.02.011"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""This study confirms several patterns that have emerged in previous work ( Bergelson & Swingley, 2012 , 2013b ; Parise & Csibra, 2012 ; Tincoff & Jusczyk, 1999 , 2012 ). Namely, we find further evidence, with a new sample of infants and a new set of item pairs, that 6-9 month olds understand words."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""While the study was not designed to test the difference between noun and verb acquisition, 6–9-month-olds did perform well on one of the two verb pairs ( hug - eat ), contrary to their poor performance on a broader set of non-nouns in a prior study ( Bergelson & Swingley, 2013 ). Whether infants’ apparent success on hug-eat represents random item variation, or a first fragile glimmer of infants’ understanding of these verbs, is unclear, particularly given infants’ failure to demonstrate understanding of kiss-drink here."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors explicitly contrast success on one verb pair with prior non-noun failure while another pair fails; they also confirm the age boost from this original. The broad sentence about confirming several patterns does not give an overall verdict on the entire replication of this paper. Agreement passage: Not unexpectedly, this ability increases with age, but perhaps less expectedly the rate with which it increases is non-linear. This is the third study that finds evidence of a boost in performance around 13-14 months, independent of word type ( Bergelson & Swingley, 2012 , 2013b ).""}"
