10.23668/psycharchives.21850,6fb03a82818463ac,1,10.1037/edu0000618,Distributions of textbook tasks predict student learning: Data from decimal arithmetic.,"Tian, J., Braithwaite, D. W., & Siegler, R. S.",2021,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Tian, J., Braithwaite, D. W., & Siegler, R. S."", ""year"": ""2021"", ""title"": ""Distributions of textbook tasks predict student learning: Data from decimal arithmetic."", ""doi"": ""10.1037/edu0000618"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Our goal was to check whether Swiss\n\nmathematics textbook series may also reveal imbalances in problem frequencies, as\n\nreported by Braithwaite and Siegler (2018) and Tian et al. (2021) for U.S. textbooks."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""These patterns largely resemble, but differ also in part from those observed in U.S.\n\ntextbooks, analyzed by Braithwaite and Siegler (2018) and Tian et al. (2021). Most notably\n\n(and to be explicitly tested in Study 2), in Swiss textbooks, children encounter +/- problems\n\ninvolving fractions with unequal denominators more frequently than those involving equal\n\ndenominators. In contrast, U.S. textbooks present these two types of in roughly equal\n\nproportions."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Study 1 confirms nonrandom decimal problem distributions but the authors also explicitly identify important distribution differences from the U.S. textbooks. The new child decimal tasks extend the finding rather than reproduce an original child experiment.""}"
