10.1111/cogs.12962,ceef0c5985f4ad4c,1,,Lexical effects on compensation for coarticulation: The ghost of Christmash past.,"Magnuson, J. S., McMurray, B., Tanenhaus, M. K., & Aslin, R. N.",2003,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Magnuson, J. S., McMurray, B., Tanenhaus, M. K., & Aslin, R. N."", ""year"": ""2003"", ""title"": ""Lexical effects on compensation for coarticulation: The ghost of Christmash past."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We observed robust LCfC when we combined items that survived our pilot criteria in our experiments: Lexically restored phonemes influenced perception of subsequent phonemes (e.g., a shift toward more  tapes  responses following  fooli#  vs. more  capes  responses following  Christma# ). Critically, participants in our experiment only ever heard ambiguous phonemes at the ends of context stimuli, suggesting that phoneme restoration was driven by the lexicon rather than by experiment‐induced biases (the explanation McQueen et al.,  2009  proposed for the results of Magnuson et al.,  2003a ). We observed LCfC effects both in an initial experiment  and  in a direct replication of the study, with effect sizes comparable to those reported by Elman and McClelland ( 1988 ) and Magnuson et al. ( 2003a )."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We observed robust LCfC when we combined items that survived our pilot criteria in our experiments: Lexically restored phonemes influenced perception of subsequent phonemes (e.g., a shift toward more  tapes  responses following  fooli#  vs. more  capes  responses following  Christma# ). Critically, participants in our experiment only ever heard ambiguous phonemes at the ends of context stimuli, suggesting that phoneme restoration was driven by the lexicon rather than by experiment‐induced biases (the explanation McQueen et al.,  2009  proposed for the results of Magnuson et al.,  2003a ). We observed LCfC effects both in an initial experiment  and  in a direct replication of the study, with effect sizes comparable to those reported by Elman and McClelland ( 1988 ) and Magnuson et al. ( 2003a )."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Both new samples show the LCfC effect reported by this target; the authors explicitly compare their observed effects to both cited papers. The differing interaction concerns the within-paper replication, not a contrasted published target finding.""}"
10.1089/cap.2024.0078,f92cb024df9f1aac,0,,"Replicating and extending the reliability, criterion validity, and treatment sensitivity of the shortened PANSS for pediatric trials.","Busner J, Youngstrom EA, Langfus JA, et al.",2024,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Busner J, Youngstrom EA, Langfus JA, et al."", ""year"": ""2024"", ""title"": ""Replicating and extending the reliability, criterion validity, and treatment sensitivity of the shortened PANSS for pediatric trials."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The results presented in this study evaluated these optimized forms in data from an additional independent randomized pediatric schizophrenia trial (Savitz et al., 2015 ). Overall, these results support three previous studies showing that the PANSS10 and PANSS20 compare favorably to the 30-item PANSS in terms of content coverage, reliability, validity, and calibration, with a significant reduction in administration length (Busner et al., 2024 ; Findling et al., 2023 ; Youngstrom et al., 2024 )."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Overall, these results support three previous studies showing that the PANSS10 and PANSS20 compare favorably to the 30-item PANSS in terms of content coverage, reliability, validity, and calibration, with a significant reduction in administration length (Busner et al., 2024 ; Findling et al., 2023 ; Youngstrom et al., 2024 )."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors state an overall supportive verdict explicitly covering all three prior studies.""}"
