10.1192/bjp.161.2.223,00bd205abdb1ee24,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,"{""target_index"": 0, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""obvious"", ""verdict"": ""undeterminable"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""Forty-two schizophrenic patients and their close relatives took part in an Italian replication study of expressed emotion (EE). The patients were selected from the psychiatric ward of a general hospital in Milan and were subsequently followed up for nine months. All patients attended a community service clinic as out-patients, and all but one were prescribed neuroleptics for the duration of the study. Relatives were assigned to the high-EE group if they scored 4 or 5 on the emotional overinvolvement (EOI) scale, or showed hostility, or made six or more critical comments. On this basis, 18 (42%) families were rated as low EE and 24 (57%) as high EE. At follow-up, the admission rate for the 9-month period was significantly higher for the high-EE group (P<0.05). Furthermore, significantly fewer patients were readmitted from families showing high warmth (P<0.05). The presence of high warmth appeared to be associated with a lower admission rate, even in high-EE families."", ""location"": ""Complete available primary abstract; independent original-identity validation where specified"", ""reason"": ""Actual primary evidence-access/identity limit: available complete abstract describes an Italian expressed-emotion replication and nine-month readmission comparisons but names no original author/year/reference. Multiple empirical EE findings predate this work; the supplied Vaughn-Leff title alone cannot uniquely identify the closest target. Source recovery ended partial, not verified full text. Two Argon replies described this actual ambiguity; bundle remains unvalidated. Parent independently checked the whole abstract and preserves the candidate unresolved, with no asserted missing original. NEW parent primary-source disposition; no rejected provider quote was repaired, no partial model reply was promoted and no attempt budget reset.""}","{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Forty-two schizophrenic patients and their close relatives took part in an Italian replication study of expressed emotion (EE)."", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""outcome_quote"": """", ""outcome_quote_section"": """", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The abstract identifies a replication but neither identifies the specific earlier paper nor states its finding or an overall replication verdict.""}"
10.1353/hub.2006.0033,6d0a7118936c5d54,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Our previous studies showed a statistically significant association between UBL5 sequence variation and several obesity- and diabetes-related quantitative physiological measures in Asian Indian and Micronesian cohorts. Here we undertake a replication study in a Mexican American cohort where the original linkage signal was first detected."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""These data are consistent with our earlier published studies and further support a functional role for the UBL5 gene in influencing physiological traits that underpin the development of metabolic syndrome."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Abstract gives a collective agreement verdict for the previous UBL5 association studies; bibliography is unavailable.""}"
10.1017/s0033291706008257,6ec9c383d4fddd12,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The present report is the second large-scale validation study of the PDSQ in psychiatric out-patients. Because some of the sizes of the diagnostic groups in the initial report were modest, it is important to determine whether the recommended cut-off scores to screen for psychiatric disorders remained the same in a replication study."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Based on receiver operating curve analysis, the PDSQ performed as well in the replication sample as in the initial validation study."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The abstract explicitly compares overall diagnostic performance with the initial validation and says it performed as well. A count of unchanged cutoff scores does not by itself overturn that overall comparison. The abstract gives no citation identifying the initial validation paper; bibliographic fields are left empty.""}"
10.1111/j.1469-1809.2009.00555.x,78b62459579bc203,0,10.1038/ng.125,Identification of ten loci associated with height highlights new biological pathways in human growth,Lettre G; Jackson AU; Gieger C; Schumacher FR; Berndt SI; Sanna S; Eyheramendy S; Voight BF; Butler JL; Guiducci C; Illig T; Hackett R; Heid IM; Jacobs KB; Lyssenko V; Uda M; Boehnke M; Chanock SJ; Groop LC; Hu FB; Isomaa B; Kraft P; Peltonen L; Salomaa V; Schlessinger D; Hunter DJ; Hayes RB; Abecasis GR; Wichmann HE; Mohlke KL; Hirschhorn JN,2008,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Lettre G; Jackson AU; Gieger C; Schumacher FR; Berndt SI; Sanna S; Eyheramendy S; Voight BF; Butler JL; Guiducci C; Illig T; Hackett R; Heid IM; Jacobs KB; Lyssenko V; Uda M; Boehnke M; Chanock SJ; Groop LC; Hu FB; Isomaa B; Kraft P; Peltonen L; Salomaa V; Schlessinger D; Hunter DJ; Hayes RB; Abecasis GR; Wichmann HE; Mohlke KL; Hirschhorn JN"", ""year"": ""2008"", ""title"": ""Identification of ten loci associated with height highlights new biological pathways in human growth"", ""doi"": ""10.1038/ng.125"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Recent GWAS reports have revealed a novel variant rs1042725 in HMGA2 for human adult height in the European populations.( Lettre et al. , 2008 , Weedon et al. , 2008 , Weedon et al. , 2007 ) Replication of GWAS findings can be quite difficult, especially for the quantitative trait. In this study, we successfully replicated this finding both in the unrelated and family-based US Caucasian populations, which demonstrated the validity of the initial finding."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In summary, using data from ~11,000 subjects, we have confirmed the recently found association between HMGA2 and human adult height even across ethnic boundaries, in both Caucasian and Chinese populations, which highlights the importance of this gene involved in normal growth."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The paper explicitly groups these three GWAS as the finding being replicated and concludes confirmation across populations.""}"
