10.1162/jocn_a_00109,b34401f4d544f44b,1,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Also, with regard to relationship between reading and spelling, we replicated previous functional neuroimaging studies that have shown overlapping regions of activation in the left posterior inferior frontal gyrus and midfusiform gyrus for word reading and spelling."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Also, with regard to relationship between reading and spelling, we replicated previous functional neuroimaging studies that have shown overlapping regions of activation in the left posterior inferior frontal gyrus and midfusiform gyrus for word reading and spelling."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors explicitly report convergence or replication of the earlier finding.""}"
10.1037/a0028072,137a8b84a4506060,1,10.1080/01690968508402073,Lexical acquisition and the modular lexicon.,"Forster, K. I.",1985,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""obvious"", ""verdict"": ""undeterminable"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""However, it might be argued that\n\nmasked repetition priming is such a robust effect that it can be\n\nobtained in any task with any population, and therefore the inter-\n\npretation rests entirely on the failure to find repetition priming for\n\nnew words with the monolingual English speakers (see Table 4).\n\nHowever, this finding has been observed at least twice before\n(Forster, 1985; Rajaram & Neely, 1992) and hence must be treated\n\nas a reliable phenomenon."", ""location"": {""start_line"": 1964, ""end_line"": 1977, ""form_feed_page"": 1}, ""reason"": ""The full current primary Experiment3 genuinely retests the native old/new masked-priming benchmark, so control-group status is not evidence of background-only use. However it attributes one shared benchmark jointly to Forster1985andRajaram/Neely1992; the latter is the closest named later empirical finding and is separately confirmed. The original primary/university abstracts corroborate a common old-item-only pattern, but current text does not identify a separate Forster-only finding being retested (its nonword analogy is not a new nonword test). Under closest-chain rule, whether Forster additionally qualifies cannot be settled just by another DOI/paper or groupedcitation. Preserve an actual shared-finding/closest-target ambiguity and list only Rajaram as confirmed, keeping Forster unresolved rather than a forced second omission/exclusion. Prior positive expert decision and all attempts retained."", ""quote_provenance"": {""kind"": ""new_independent_literal_primary_quotation"", ""issue_index"": 163, ""source_sha256"": ""af9a01d0be92a1d38441468d566f4e82c9ba949783fff080ff1664520942f190"", ""raw_start_offset"": 62944, ""raw_end_offset"": 63401, ""inspection_result"": ""evidence_followup/integration/strict_literal_quote_inspection.result.json""}}","{""authors"": ""Forster, K. I."", ""year"": ""1985"", ""title"": ""Lexical acquisition and the modular lexicon."", ""doi"": ""10.1080/01690968508402073"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""3"", ""evidence_quote"": ""In this final experiment, a more powerful test of the episodic L2 hypothesis is carried out using a more robust form of priming, namely, repetition priming rather than translation priming. As explained earlier, it has been found that in an episodic recognition task, old words show strong masked repetition priming, but there is no priming for new words (Forster, 1985; Rajaram & Neely, 1992)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The findings are clear. In an episodic recognition task, Chinese– English bilinguals show repetition priming for both old and new English (L2) words. This contrasts sharply with the results for the monolingual English speakers, where repetition priming was ob- tained for old words only."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true}"
10.1027/1614-0001/a000082,ec1e42c67e9ae963,1,,Adolescents’ music preferences and personality characteristics.,"Delsing, M. J., Ter Bogt, T. F. M., Engels, R. C., & Meeus, W. H.",2008,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Delsing, M. J., Ter Bogt, T. F. M., Engels, R. C., & Meeus, W. H."", ""year"": ""2008"", ""title"": ""Adolescents’ music preferences and personality characteristics."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Therefore, we compared the correlations computed\nbased on mean scores to previous findings (Table 5), which\nshowed by and large similar patterns and correlations as in\nprevious studies (Delsing et al., 2008; George et al., 2007;\nRentfrow & Gosling, 2003; Zweigenhaft, 2008)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""All in all, the results of the studies from the United States\nand The Netherlands regarding the factor structure of the\nSTOMP dimensions as well as the correlations between\nmusic preferences and personality also apply to the German\nsample."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The paper explicitly compares the same published personality/music correlations in new German data. Its overall verdict states these prior findings apply to the German sample; discussion names this paper within the set described as generally consistent.""}"
