10.1080/23279095.2021.1917411,a6810e2bb8d99849,0,,,Jones et al.,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Jones et al."", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""This project was a systematic replication of a study conducted by Jones et al. using a demographically-different sample of participants."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""This finding suggests that the original regression equation created by Jones et al. can be applied to the current sample to accurately predict reading level classification."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors conclude that the original prediction equation applies accurately in the new sample; a different raw-score distribution does not overturn that central result.""}"
10.1186/s12886-022-02359-1,d025a0502d173534,1,10.1136/bjophthalmol-2018-312218,Analysis of multiple genetic loci reveals MPDZ-NF1B rs1324183 as a putative genetic marker for keratoconus.,"Wang YM, Ma L, Lu SY, Chan TCY, Yam JCS, Tang SM, et al.",2018,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Wang YM, Ma L, Lu SY, Chan TCY, Yam JCS, Tang SM, et al."", ""year"": ""2018"", ""title"": ""Analysis of multiple genetic loci reveals MPDZ-NF1B rs1324183 as a putative genetic marker for keratoconus."", ""doi"": ""10.1136/bjophthalmol-2018-312218"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""In recently years, Wang YM and Hao XD et al. both analyzed the association of SNP locus with keratoconus in Chinese population, and revealed that the rs1324183 (\nMPDZ-NF1B\n), which located between the\nMPDZ\nand\nNF1B\ngenes, is a putative genetic marker for monitoring the progression of keratoconus and should be further investigated in other different Chinese populations [\n23\n,\n24\n]. Thence, in this study, we collected a total of 114 KC patients who live in northwestern of China for the first time and further confirmed the association between the rs1324183 (\nMPDZ-NF1B\n) and KC, providing a clear basis for early clinical diagnosis in Chinese population."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Notably, many studies have reported that the SNP rs1324183 (\nMPDZ-NFIB\n) is a genetic marker highly correlated with KC cases [\n23\n,\n24\n,\n30\n,\n31\n,\n33\n], and in our population, we have also further confirmed that the genotype AA of rs1324183 confers a higher risk for KC (Table\n2\n)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors explicitly confirm the shared SNP–KC association and risk genotype in their independent Chinese sample.""}"
10.1016/j.ynirp.2022.100149,8d5eccbe2686d4da,0,10.1038/s41380-020-0754-0,Brain aging in major depressive disorder: results from the ENIGMA major depressive disorder working group.,"Han L.K.M., Dinga R., Hahn T., Ching C.R.K., Eyler L.T., Aftanas L., Aghajani M., Aleman A., Baune B.T., Berger K., Brak I., Filho G.B., Carballedo A., Connolly C.G., Couvy-Duchesne B., Cullen K.R., Dannlowski U., Davey C.G., Dima D., Duran F.L.S., Enneking V., Filimonova E., Frenzel S., Frodl T., Fu C.H.Y., Godlewska B.R., Gotlib I.H., Grabe H.J., Groenewold N.A., Grotegerd D., Gruber O., Hall G.B., Harrison B.J., Hatton S.N., Hermesdorf M., Hickie I.B., Ho T.C., Hosten N., Jansen A., Kähler C., Kircher T., Klimes-Dougan B., Krämer B., Krug A., Lagopoulos J., Leenings R., MacMaster F.P., MacQueen G., McIntosh A., McLellan Q., McMahon K.L., Medland S.E., Mueller B.A., Mwangi B., Osipov E., Portella M.J., Pozzi E., Reneman L., Repple J., Rosa P.G.P., Sacchet M.D., Sämann P.G., Schnell K., Schrantee A., Simulionyte E., Soares J.C., Sommer J., Stein D.J., Steinsträter O., Strike L.T., Thomopoulos S.I., van Tol M.-J., Veer I.M., Vermeiren R.R.J.M., Walter H., van der Wee N.J.A., van der Werff S.J.A., Whalley H., Winter N.R., Wittfeld K., Wright M.J., Wu M.-J., Völzke H., Yang T.T., Zannias V., de Zubicaray G.I., Zunta-Soares G.B., Abé C., Alda M., Andreassen O.A., Bøen E., Bonnin C.M., Canales-Rodriguez E.J., Cannon D., Caseras X., Chaim-Avancini T.M., Elvsåshagen T., Favre P., Foley S.F., Fullerton J.M., Goikolea J.M., Haarman B.C.M., Hajek T., Henry C., Houenou J., Howells F.M., Ingvar M., Kuplicki R., Lafer B., Landén M., Machado-Vieira R., Malt U.F., McDonald C., Mitchell P.B., Nabulsi L., Otaduy M.C.G., Overs B.J., Polosan M., Pomarol-Clotet E., Radua J., Rive M.M., Roberts G., Ruhe H.G., Salvador R., Sarró S., Satterthwaite T.D., Savitz J., Schene A.H., Schofield P.R., Serpa M.H., Sim K., Soeiro-de-Souza M.G., Sutherland A.N., Temmingh H.S., Timmons G.M., Uhlmann A., Vieta E., Wolf D.H., Zanetti M.V., Jahanshad N., Thompson P.M., Veltman D.J., Penninx B.W.J.H., Marquand A.F., Cole J.H., Schmaal L.",2021,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Han L.K.M., Dinga R., Hahn T., Ching C.R.K., Eyler L.T., Aftanas L., Aghajani M., Aleman A., Baune B.T., Berger K., Brak I., Filho G.B., Carballedo A., Connolly C.G., Couvy-Duchesne B., Cullen K.R., Dannlowski U., Davey C.G., Dima D., Duran F.L.S., Enneking V., Filimonova E., Frenzel S., Frodl T., Fu C.H.Y., Godlewska B.R., Gotlib I.H., Grabe H.J., Groenewold N.A., Grotegerd D., Gruber O., Hall G.B., Harrison B.J., Hatton S.N., Hermesdorf M., Hickie I.B., Ho T.C., Hosten N., Jansen A., Kähler C., Kircher T., Klimes-Dougan B., Krämer B., Krug A., Lagopoulos J., Leenings R., MacMaster F.P., MacQueen G., McIntosh A., McLellan Q., McMahon K.L., Medland S.E., Mueller B.A., Mwangi B., Osipov E., Portella M.J., Pozzi E., Reneman L., Repple J., Rosa P.G.P., Sacchet M.D., Sämann P.G., Schnell K., Schrantee A., Simulionyte E., Soares J.C., Sommer J., Stein D.J., Steinsträter O., Strike L.T., Thomopoulos S.I., van Tol M.-J., Veer I.M., Vermeiren R.R.J.M., Walter H., van der Wee N.J.A., van der Werff S.J.A., Whalley H., Winter N.R., Wittfeld K., Wright M.J., Wu M.-J., Völzke H., Yang T.T., Zannias V., de Zubicaray G.I., Zunta-Soares G.B., Abé C., Alda M., Andreassen O.A., Bøen E., Bonnin C.M., Canales-Rodriguez E.J., Cannon D., Caseras X., Chaim-Avancini T.M., Elvsåshagen T., Favre P., Foley S.F., Fullerton J.M., Goikolea J.M., Haarman B.C.M., Hajek T., Henry C., Houenou J., Howells F.M., Ingvar M., Kuplicki R., Lafer B., Landén M., Machado-Vieira R., Malt U.F., McDonald C., Mitchell P.B., Nabulsi L., Otaduy M.C.G., Overs B.J., Polosan M., Pomarol-Clotet E., Radua J., Rive M.M., Roberts G., Ruhe H.G., Salvador R., Sarró S., Satterthwaite T.D., Savitz J., Schene A.H., Schofield P.R., Serpa M.H., Sim K., Soeiro-de-Souza M.G., Sutherland A.N., Temmingh H.S., Timmons G.M., Uhlmann A., Vieta E., Wolf D.H., Zanetti M.V., Jahanshad N., Thompson P.M., Veltman D.J., Penninx B.W.J.H., Marquand A.F., Cole J.H., Schmaal L."", ""year"": ""2021"", ""title"": ""Brain aging in major depressive disorder: results from the ENIGMA major depressive disorder working group."", ""doi"": ""10.1038/s41380-020-0754-0"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Our previous ENIGMA MDD consortium study, the largest study of brain age in depression to date (N = 2126 controls and N = 2675 patients), showed a +1.08 year higher brain-PAD in depression (Cohen's d = 0.14), but with no evidence that this effect was driven by specific (clinical) characteristics such as age, age of onset, recurrence status, remission status, or antidepressant use (\nHan et al., 2021a\n). The subsequent addition of new cohorts to the ENIGMA MDD consortium since our previous brain age study provides us with an unique opportunity to perform an independent replication study in new data to validate our developed algorithm, as well as determine whether depression is consistently associated with older appearing brains (\nWrigglesworth et al., 2021\n)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""This replication study using data from 13 cohorts around the world confirmed our previous findings that persons with major depressive disorder show advanced brain aging compared to controls by approximately +1 year."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Overall conclusion explicitly confirms the original brain-age gap using new independent cohorts. Cohort heterogeneity does not override that verdict.""}"
10.1002/jaba.908,7d4203391941be65,1,10.1901/jaba.1995.28-317,Training residential supervisors to provide feedback for maintaining staff teaching skills with people who have severe disabilities.,"Parsons, M. B., & Reid, D. H.",1995,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Parsons, M. B., & Reid, D. H."", ""year"": ""1995"", ""title"": ""Training residential supervisors to provide feedback for maintaining staff teaching skills with people who have severe disabilities."", ""doi"": ""10.1901/jaba.1995.28-317"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""The results of Study 2 extend ﬁndings of\nprevious studies (Parsons & Reid, 1995;\nShuler & Carroll, 2019), by demonstrating that\ntherapists’\nguided-compliance\nintegrity\nincreased after receiving performance feedback\nfrom one of the supervisors trained in Study\n1. All three therapists in Study 2 reached our\nmastery criterion following three to six feedback sessions (M = four sessions)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The results of Study 2 extend ﬁndings of\nprevious studies (Parsons & Reid, 1995;\nShuler & Carroll, 2019), by demonstrating that\ntherapists’\nguided-compliance\nintegrity\nincreased after receiving performance feedback\nfrom one of the supervisors trained in Study\n1. All three therapists in Study 2 reached our\nmastery criterion following three to six feedback sessions (M = four sessions)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true}"
10.1073/pnas.2202700119,67cac39d6ecd7223,1,,Experimental investigation of the subjective value of information in trading.,"Rafaeli S., Raban D.",2003,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""authors"": ""Rafaeli S., Raban D."", ""year"": ""2003"", ""title"": ""Experimental investigation of the subjective value of information in trading."", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""The current research is closest to a set of studies in information science conducted by Rafaeli and Raban (27, 28), who replicated the endowment effect for information in a game mimicking sales decisions. They gave participants the opportunity to buy and sell information, and observed greater willingness-to-accept prices than willingness-to-pay amounts. We extend their work in several critical ways."", ""outcome_quote"": ""However, we predicted that participants endowed with the three-fact bundle would be less likely than nonendowed participants to choose the four-fact bundle, because doing so would feel to them like a loss—rather than foregone gain—of three facts. Indeed, participants in the endowed condition were more likely to prefer learning just three facts (68%) than those in the nonendowed condition (46%) (χ2(1, n = 146) = 7.03, P = 0.008, Φ = 0.219) (Fig. 2)."", ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The paper explicitly extends the cited information-endowment experiments, addressing their monetary and instrumental confounds. Study 2 finds the predicted ownership preference in noninstrumental information, agreeing with their endowment finding. Target relation is conceptual and somewhat less explicit than the Asian Disease target.""}"
10.1073/pnas.2202700119,67cac39d6ecd7223,2,,The effect of source nature and status on the subjective value of information.,"Raban D. R., Rafaeli S.",2006,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""authors"": ""Raban D. R., Rafaeli S."", ""year"": ""2006"", ""title"": ""The effect of source nature and status on the subjective value of information."", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""The current research is closest to a set of studies in information science conducted by Rafaeli and Raban (27, 28), who replicated the endowment effect for information in a game mimicking sales decisions. They gave participants the opportunity to buy and sell information, and observed greater willingness-to-accept prices than willingness-to-pay amounts. We extend their work in several critical ways."", ""outcome_quote"": ""However, we predicted that participants endowed with the three-fact bundle would be less likely than nonendowed participants to choose the four-fact bundle, because doing so would feel to them like a loss—rather than foregone gain—of three facts. Indeed, participants in the endowed condition were more likely to prefer learning just three facts (68%) than those in the nonendowed condition (46%) (χ2(1, n = 146) = 7.03, P = 0.008, Φ = 0.219) (Fig. 2)."", ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The paper explicitly extends the cited information-endowment experiments, addressing their monetary and instrumental confounds. Study 2 finds the predicted ownership preference in noninstrumental information, agreeing with their endowment finding. Target relation is conceptual and somewhat less explicit than the Asian Disease target.""}"
10.1002/acp.4037,7086ab0209b0caf9,0,10.1002/acp.3301,Why education predicts decreased belief in conspiracy theories,"van Prooijen, J.-W.",2017,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""van Prooijen, J.-W."", ""year"": ""2017"", ""title"": ""Why education predicts decreased belief in conspiracy theories"", ""doi"": ""10.1002/acp.3301"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""The main aim of the study was to replicate and extend van Prooijen's findings on how education and its outcomes (cognitive complexity, subjective social standing, self‐esteem, a feeling of control and powerlessness, cognitive reflection, epistemic curiosity and scientific reasoning) predict conspiracy beliefs."", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""outcome_quote"": """", ""outcome_quote_section"": """", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The abstract identifies the target and reports the present mediators and negative education association, but does not state which original mediator findings were confirmed or contradicted or supply an overall replication verdict. A shared education association alone cannot settle the replication of the multiple targeted pathways.""}"
10.1016/j.psychres.2024.115855,d39b63aa6fc0aca2,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Recently, we found hypomethylation of OXT in patients with Major Depressive Disorder (MDD) compared to healthy controls (HC). Since the replicability of findings is a key point of criticism in (epi‑)genetic research, we aimed to confirm our previous findings in a pre-registered study (data was stored in a database prior to pre-registration) within a new sample of n = 85 patients with MDD and n = 85 HC."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In accordance with our previous study, we found hypomethylation of OXT in patients with MDD compared to HC."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The abstract explicitly confirms the original patient-control hypomethylation finding. The earlier paper is not identified by citation in the abstract.""}"
10.1098/rsos.250290,db1ca79d149b328f,1,10.1126/science.1253932,Avoiding overhead aversion in charity,"Gneezy U , Keenan EA , Gneezy A",2014,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Gneezy U , Keenan EA , Gneezy A"", ""year"": ""2014"", ""title"": ""Avoiding overhead aversion in charity"", ""doi"": ""10.1126/science.1253932"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Hypothesis 7, which found that participants were biased towards a charity where overhead is paid for by another donor even when cost-effectiveness is kept constant, acts as a conceptual replication of Gneezy et al . [ 16 ]."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Hypothesis 7, which found that participants were biased towards a charity where overhead is paid for by another donor even when cost-effectiveness is kept constant, acts as a conceptual replication of Gneezy et al . [ 16 ]. In contrast to the between-subject design that they used in their study, our extension shows that this effect occurs in a within-subject context as well."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors explicitly confirm the replicated finding; the overall verdict governs the Baron and Szymanska target.""}"
10.1108/scm-06-2023-0297,9de69025f12b33a8,0,,,Ambulkar et al.,2015,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Ambulkar et al."", ""year"": ""2015"", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The purpose is to replicate and extend Ambulkar et al.’s (2015) work testing resource reconfiguration as a mediator of the supply chain disruption/firm resilience relationship and testing risk management infrastructure as a moderator."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Two of four of Ambulkars et al.’s (2015) hypotheses were confirmed as relevant to firm resilience during the pandemic while two were not confirmed."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Explicit partial confirmation of the four hypotheses from a single original paper. Original reference citation is unavailable in abstract.""}"
10.1177/0022022110381114,3531eb735322f77d,2,,Self-awareness in human and chimpanzee infants: What is measured and what is meant by the mark and mirror test?,"Bard, K. A., Todd, B. K., Bernier, C., Love, J., & Leavens, D. A.",2006,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,"{""request_id"": ""scope_3531eb735322f77d_126"", ""file_key"": ""3531eb735322f77d"", ""target_index"": 2, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""undeterminable"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""In 2006, Bard et al. compared spontaneous and\nprompted self-orienting behaviors of children in the mark test and reported that maternal prompt-\ning increased self-referential verbal labeling. Although no statistical tests were applied (n = 4),\nthese findings do suggest that verbal prompts may be needed to encourage self-referential behav-\nior and labeling (Bard et al., 2006)."", ""location"": ""General Discussion, p. 9 (also Introduction, p. 2)"", ""reason"": ""In the Introduction (p. 2), Broesch et al. (2010) cite Bard, Todd, Bernier, Love, & Leavens (2006; Infancy, 9(2), 191–219, DOI 10.1207/s15327078in0902_6) among eight studies attributed with the 18-to-24-month Western MSR pass-rate finding (~50% at 18 months and >70% at 24 months), whereas in the General Discussion (p. 9) they describe Bard et al. (2006) in terms of an n = 4 comparison of spontaneous versus maternally prompted self-orienting and verbal self-labeling. The supplied original source record for Bard et al. (2006) contains only the primary author abstract (level: primary_author_abstract), which confirms that the article reports a primary study with human infants comparing developmental and comparative mark-test procedures and maternal scaffolding in addition to the prompting subanalysis, but does not provide the full-text methods, infant age range, or unprompted mark-directed pass rates by age. Without the full text of Bard et al. (2006), the available evidence cannot settle the precise unresolved question of whether Bard et al.’s primary human-infant study empirically established the 18-to-24-month spontaneous mark-directed pass-rate benchmark retested by Broesch et al. (2010) or only tested procedural/scaffolding variations.""}","{""authors"": ""Bard, K. A., Todd, B. K., Bernier, C., Love, J., & Leavens, D. A."", ""year"": ""2006"", ""title"": ""Self-awareness in human and chimpanzee infants: What is measured and what is meant by the mark and mirror test?"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Numerous developmental studies of predominantly urban Western middle-class children suggest that children reach this milestone by 18 to 24 months of age. By 18 months, approximately 50% pass and by 24 months a significant majority of children (more than 70%) are reported to self- refer by touching or removing a mark from their own body (face or other body region) while exploring their mirror image (Amsterdam, 1972; Asendorpf, Warkentin, & Baudonnière, 1996; Bard, Todd, Bernier, Love, & Leavens, 2006; Bertenthal & Fisher, 1978; Lewis & Brooks-Gunn, 1979; Lewis & Ramsay, 2004; Nielsen, Dissanayake, & Kashima, 2003; Schulman & Kaplowitz, 1976). Here we report cross-cultural observations on the emergence of mirror self-recognition (MSR), using a simplified mark test, raising questions regarding the meaning and universal validity of this test as an ontogenetic benchmark of self-concept. The basic rationale behind the mark test is that when a child is marked surreptitiously and attempts to touch the mark on the body, as opposed to doing nothing or trying to reach for it on the mirror surface, it indicates that the specular image stands for the child’s own body. Self-oriented gestures are taken as the behavioral index of self-recognition, indicating that the embodied self is the referent of what is seen in the mirror (Rochat, 2003). In a different account, Nielsen, Suddendorf, and Slaughter (2006) claim that the mark test is passed by individuals who have a rapidly updatable image of themselves that they recognize in the mirror. Regardless of the rela- tive validity of these accounts, the general consensus is that passing the mark test indicates that children recognize their image and that those who fail the test do not. From a comparative perspective, chimpanzees, orangutans, dolphins, and Asian elephants are among the few nonhuman species reported to pass the mark test (Gallup, 1982; Plotnik & de Waal, 2006; Povinelli, 1995; Reiss & Marino, 1998). Nonhuman individuals passing the test typically have extensive prior experience with reflective surfaces. In contrast, research with humans suggests that children’s relative familiarity with mirrors, which greatly varies across contexts, does not correlate with the age at which the mark test is passed (Priel & deSchonen, 1986). Priel and deShonen (1986) tested Bedouin nomadic children with no previous mirror experience and compared them to same-age Israeli children familiar with mirrors. They found no significant difference in the developmental onset of MSR between mirror familiar and unfa- miliar children. However, more recent cross-cultural studies point to significant cultural variations in the onset of MSR. Keller and collaborators compared 18- to 20-month-olds from urban Greece, Costa Rica, Germany, as well as from a rural community in Cameroon, and they report a greater proportion of German, Greek, and Costa Rican children passing the test (more than 50%), compared to Cameroonian children (less than 4%) (Keller, Kartner, Borke, Yovsi, & Kleis, 2005; Keller et al., 2004). These authors correlate such variations to variations in par- enting strategies that exist across these cultures, fostering more or less autonomy in the young child. If such variability is validated with other cultures, testing children older than 18 to 20 months would allow us to determine whether this variability pertains only to a developmental delay, pos- sibly resulting from variations in parenting styles (see Keller et al., 2005; Keller et al., 2004) or whether such differences persist throughout development. If the latter is the case, this would sug- gest that there may be culture-specific responses to the passing of the mirror mark test (i.e., greater occurrence of potential false negatives). To probe this alternative explanation, we per- formed two experiments testing (a) whether cross-cultural variability in the passing of the mirror mark test could be verified across more cultures and (b) whether such variability would persist beyond the age at which children typically are reported to pass the mirror mark test. In two experiments, we tested children 18 to 72 months old living in seven distinct cultural groups. Because of the exploratory nature of this work, aside from probing these questions, no specific hypotheses were made."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The second experiment demonstrates that a significant amount of variability exists across cultural contexts in children’s behavioral response to this version of the MSR test that measures spontaneous self-recognition (see also Lewis & Ramsey, 2004). Consistent with past research, 77% to 88% of Western children (Canada and United States) pass this version of the MSR test by spontaneously displaying self-oriented behaviors. In contrast, only half of the children from Saint Lucia, Grenada, or Peru and none of the Fijian children of the same age showed any sign of such behaviors."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Western samples agree with the cited developmental benchmark; non-Western samples explicitly contrast with it. No overall replication verdict supersedes these comparisons.""}"
10.1177/0022022110381114,3531eb735322f77d,7,,Mirror-image response during the first two years of life.,"Schulman, A. H., & Kaplowitz, C.",1976,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,"{""request_id"": ""scope_3531eb735322f77d_7"", ""file_key"": ""3531eb735322f77d"", ""target_index"": 7, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""undeterminable"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""Numerous developmental studies of predominantly urban Western middle-class children suggest\nthat children reach this milestone by 18 to 24 months of age. By 18 months, approximately 50%\npass and by 24 months a significant majority of children (more than 70%) are reported to self-\nrefer by touching or removing a mark from their own body (face or other body region) while\nexploring their mirror image (Amsterdam, 1972; Asendorpf, Warkentin, & Baudonnière, 1996;\nBard, Todd, Bernier, Love, & Leavens, 2006; Bertenthal & Fisher, 1978; Lewis & Brooks-Gunn,\n1979; Lewis & Ramsay, 2004; Nielsen, Dissanayake, & Kashima, 2003; Schulman & Kaplowitz,\n1976). Here we report cross-cultural observations on the emergence of mirror self-recognition\n(MSR), using a simplified mark test, raising questions regarding the meaning and universal\nvalidity of this test as an ontogenetic benchmark of self-concept."", ""location"": ""Introduction, p. 2; cf. Method (Dependent Measure, Analysis, and Reliability, p. 4), Experiment 1 Results and Discussion (p. 5), General Discussion (pp. 8-9), and References (p. 12)"", ""reason"": ""Addressing the unresolved scope objection: citation location or frequency alone cannot establish background status, nor can inclusion in an introductory citation cluster automatically establish a retested target. On p. 2, Broesch et al. (2010) cite Schulman and Kaplowitz (1976, \""Mirror-image response during the first two years of life\"", Developmental Psychobiology, 10, 133-142) solely inside an eight-study omnibus parenthetical supporting the summary statement that by 18 months ~50% of Western children pass and by 24 months >70% self-refer by touching or removing a mark on their body. Primary sources supplied for sibling targets in that same eight-study cluster (asendorpf1996.txt for target_index 1, bard2006.txt for target_index 2, and nielsen2003.txt for target_index 6) demonstrate that this cluster pools heterogeneous works—for example, Asendorpf et al. (1996) tested synchronic imitation only at 18 months and Bard et al. (2006) tested procedural/scaffolding variations—rather than eight uniform benchmark reports. Conversely, for the retained benchmark studies, Broesch et al. (2010) explicitly state the specific empirical findings tested by their 18-to-72-month cross-cultural mark-test experiments: Amsterdam (1972; shipped input_row_index 843), Bertenthal and Fisher (1978; target_index 3), and Lewis and Brooks-Gunn (1979; target_index 4) are explicitly identified as reporting that >60% (60% to 85%) of Western middle-class children older than 20 months pass the mark test, which directly defines the .60 probability threshold tested in the one-tailed binomial analyses of Experiments 1 and 2 (Method, p. 4; General Discussion, p. 8), and Lewis and Ramsay (2004; target_index 5) is explicitly identified as reporting >80% passing by 21 months and 100% by 24 months using a comparable spontaneous mark-test procedure (Results, p. 5; General Discussion, p. 9). Unlike those retained studies, Broesch et al. (2010) never state an individual empirical finding from Schulman and Kaplowitz (1976), and unlike targets 1, 2, and 6, no primary text or abstract of Schulman and Kaplowitz (1976) is present in the supplied evidence. The precise unresolved question is whether Schulman and Kaplowitz (1976) itself reported the specific 18-to-24-month mark-directed self-recognition pass-rate finding retested in Experiments 1 and 2 or was cited only for general developmental background on infant mirror-image responses.""}","{""authors"": ""Schulman, A. H., & Kaplowitz, C."", ""year"": ""1976"", ""title"": ""Mirror-image response during the first two years of life."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Numerous developmental studies of predominantly urban Western middle-class children suggest that children reach this milestone by 18 to 24 months of age. By 18 months, approximately 50% pass and by 24 months a significant majority of children (more than 70%) are reported to self- refer by touching or removing a mark from their own body (face or other body region) while exploring their mirror image (Amsterdam, 1972; Asendorpf, Warkentin, & Baudonnière, 1996; Bard, Todd, Bernier, Love, & Leavens, 2006; Bertenthal & Fisher, 1978; Lewis & Brooks-Gunn, 1979; Lewis & Ramsay, 2004; Nielsen, Dissanayake, & Kashima, 2003; Schulman & Kaplowitz, 1976). Here we report cross-cultural observations on the emergence of mirror self-recognition (MSR), using a simplified mark test, raising questions regarding the meaning and universal validity of this test as an ontogenetic benchmark of self-concept. The basic rationale behind the mark test is that when a child is marked surreptitiously and attempts to touch the mark on the body, as opposed to doing nothing or trying to reach for it on the mirror surface, it indicates that the specular image stands for the child’s own body. Self-oriented gestures are taken as the behavioral index of self-recognition, indicating that the embodied self is the referent of what is seen in the mirror (Rochat, 2003). In a different account, Nielsen, Suddendorf, and Slaughter (2006) claim that the mark test is passed by individuals who have a rapidly updatable image of themselves that they recognize in the mirror. Regardless of the rela- tive validity of these accounts, the general consensus is that passing the mark test indicates that children recognize their image and that those who fail the test do not. From a comparative perspective, chimpanzees, orangutans, dolphins, and Asian elephants are among the few nonhuman species reported to pass the mark test (Gallup, 1982; Plotnik & de Waal, 2006; Povinelli, 1995; Reiss & Marino, 1998). Nonhuman individuals passing the test typically have extensive prior experience with reflective surfaces. In contrast, research with humans suggests that children’s relative familiarity with mirrors, which greatly varies across contexts, does not correlate with the age at which the mark test is passed (Priel & deSchonen, 1986). Priel and deShonen (1986) tested Bedouin nomadic children with no previous mirror experience and compared them to same-age Israeli children familiar with mirrors. They found no significant difference in the developmental onset of MSR between mirror familiar and unfa- miliar children. However, more recent cross-cultural studies point to significant cultural variations in the onset of MSR. Keller and collaborators compared 18- to 20-month-olds from urban Greece, Costa Rica, Germany, as well as from a rural community in Cameroon, and they report a greater proportion of German, Greek, and Costa Rican children passing the test (more than 50%), compared to Cameroonian children (less than 4%) (Keller, Kartner, Borke, Yovsi, & Kleis, 2005; Keller et al., 2004). These authors correlate such variations to variations in par- enting strategies that exist across these cultures, fostering more or less autonomy in the young child. If such variability is validated with other cultures, testing children older than 18 to 20 months would allow us to determine whether this variability pertains only to a developmental delay, pos- sibly resulting from variations in parenting styles (see Keller et al., 2005; Keller et al., 2004) or whether such differences persist throughout development. If the latter is the case, this would sug- gest that there may be culture-specific responses to the passing of the mirror mark test (i.e., greater occurrence of potential false negatives). To probe this alternative explanation, we per- formed two experiments testing (a) whether cross-cultural variability in the passing of the mirror mark test could be verified across more cultures and (b) whether such variability would persist beyond the age at which children typically are reported to pass the mirror mark test. In two experiments, we tested children 18 to 72 months old living in seven distinct cultural groups. Because of the exploratory nature of this work, aside from probing these questions, no specific hypotheses were made."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The second experiment demonstrates that a significant amount of variability exists across cultural contexts in children’s behavioral response to this version of the MSR test that measures spontaneous self-recognition (see also Lewis & Ramsey, 2004). Consistent with past research, 77% to 88% of Western children (Canada and United States) pass this version of the MSR test by spontaneously displaying self-oriented behaviors. In contrast, only half of the children from Saint Lucia, Grenada, or Peru and none of the Fijian children of the same age showed any sign of such behaviors."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Western samples agree with the cited developmental benchmark; non-Western samples explicitly contrast with it. No overall replication verdict supersedes these comparisons.""}"
10.1523/eneuro.0332-25.2025,5c0e372f71ca86f5,1,10.1126/science.7256273,Phenomenological space-time: toward an experiential relativity.,DeLong AJ,1981,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""DeLong AJ"", ""year"": ""1981"", ""title"": ""Phenomenological space-time: toward an experiential relativity."", ""doi"": ""10.1126/science.7256273"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""3"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Second, the size of the VR did not significantly impact the retrospective time estimation as would have been expected through other studies ( DeLong, 1981 ; Riemer et al., 2014 )."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Second, the size of the VR did not significantly impact the retrospective time estimation as would have been expected through other studies ( DeLong, 1981 ; Riemer et al., 2014 ). Two differences can explain this incongruence: our study tested a different time scale and used a retrospective, not a prospective paradigm."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Experiment 3 explicitly tested whether environmental size affects time estimation and contrasts its null effect with this cited earlier result.""}"
10.1523/eneuro.0332-25.2025,5c0e372f71ca86f5,2,10.1007/s00221-014-3848-6,Interrelations between the perception of time and space in large-scale environments.,"Riemer M, Hölzl R, Kleinböhl D",2014,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Riemer M, Hölzl R, Kleinböhl D"", ""year"": ""2014"", ""title"": ""Interrelations between the perception of time and space in large-scale environments."", ""doi"": ""10.1007/s00221-014-3848-6"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""3"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Second, the size of the VR did not significantly impact the retrospective time estimation as would have been expected through other studies ( DeLong, 1981 ; Riemer et al., 2014 )."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Second, the size of the VR did not significantly impact the retrospective time estimation as would have been expected through other studies ( DeLong, 1981 ; Riemer et al., 2014 ). Two differences can explain this incongruence: our study tested a different time scale and used a retrospective, not a prospective paradigm."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The same explicit contrast identifies this independent earlier paper as a target of the environmental-size test.""}"
10.1111/lang.12403,a58225dce84de1c5,0,10.1016/j.neuropsychologia.2015.10.020,Tracking lexical consolidation with ERPs: Lexical and semantic-priming effects on N400 and LPC responses to newly-learned words.,"Bakker, I., Takashima, A., vanHell, J. G., Janzen, G., & McQueen, J. M.",2015,full_text,same_author_year_siblings,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Bakker, I., Takashima, A., vanHell, J. G., Janzen, G., & McQueen, J. M."", ""year"": ""2015"", ""title"": ""Tracking lexical consolidation with ERPs: Lexical and semantic-priming effects on N400 and LPC responses to newly-learned words."", ""doi"": ""10.1016/j.neuropsychologia.2015.10.020"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Taken together, the current study sought to conceptually replicate and extend the findings of Bakker et al. (2015), and investigate novel vocabulary learning in monolingual English speakers with minimal foreign language learning experience."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Both types of learners showed a LPC semantic priming effect (indexing controlled semantic retrieval), but no N400 effect (indexing at least partial automatic semantic retrieval), after a 24-hour consolidation period. The main difference is that only the experienced learners (Bakker et al., 2015) also showed a frontal LPC priming effect shortly after learning (for words trained and tested on Day 2)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors explicitly compare agreement after 24 hours with absence of the original immediate LPC effect. No overall replication verdict overrides these comparisons.""}"
10.1371/journal.pone.0229161,b74519d198cc668b,1,10.1177/0361684315580125,From sex objects to human beings: Masking sexual body parts and humanization as moderators to women’s objectification,"Bernard, P., Gervais, S. J., Allen, J., Delmée, A., & Klein, O.",2015,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Bernard, P., Gervais, S. J., Allen, J., Delmée, A., & Klein, O."", ""year"": ""2015"", ""title"": ""From sex objects to human beings: Masking sexual body parts and humanization as moderators to women’s objectification"", ""doi"": ""10.1177/0361684315580125"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2a"", ""evidence_quote"": ""We conducted three studies: a direct replication of Bernard and colleagues’ study [\n4\n], using their original materials, but with counterbalanced stimuli as in [\n5\n] (Study 1); a conceptual replication–to our knowledge the first one–with different sexualized materials to investigate whether the same pattern of results would emerge with different stimuli (Study 2a; indeed, so far most of the evidence in favor of the IBRT has emerged from research using the set of stimuli developed by Bernard and colleagues [\n4\n]); an investigation of the inversion effect with non-sexualized stimuli (Study 2b).\nWe expected (H1) stronger inversion effects for male than female targets and (H1b) that the participant’s gender would not moderate this difference between male and female targets. The confirmation of this hypothesis in Study 1 would be a direct replication of [\n5\n], and its confirmation in Study 2a would be a conceptual replication, enhancing its external validity beyond the specific stimuli. Study 2b tested a boundary condition, namely, whether differences in inversion effects between male and female targets would emerge also for non-sexualized stimuli."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Study 1 replicated the original results, showing a stronger inversion effect for male photos. However, no difference between male and female stimuli emerged in either Study 2a or 2b."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Direct replication agrees, conceptual replication fails; authors explicitly contrast these results. The validity critique does not remove the explicit close/conceptual mixed pattern.""}"
10.3102/0162373720948884,6f1653adad3488ee,1,,"Selection into Identification in Fixed Effects Models, with Application to Head Start","Miller, D. L., Shenhav, N. A., & Grosz, M. Z.",2019,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""outcome_computation"": ""computationally reproducible"", ""outcome_computational_quote"": ""These results closely replicate MSG (2019) findings and can be found in Table S16 of the online appendix."", ""outcome_robustness"": ""not checked"", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": ""We also performed the MSG (2019) reweighting-on-observables procedure for the Deming cohort sample. Similar to MSG (2019), we found reweighting attenuated the FFE estimates of Head Start for high school graduation, idleness, learning disability and poor health, but find little change on other young adult outcomes or ASI."", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""authors"": ""Miller, D. L., Shenhav, N. A., & Grosz, M. Z."", ""year"": ""2019"", ""title"": ""Selection into Identification in Fixed Effects Models, with Application to Head Start"", ""evidence_quote"": ""We also performed the MSG (2019) reweighting-on-observables procedure for the Deming cohort sample."", ""outcome"": """", ""outcome_quote"": """", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Table S16 directly compares MSG weighted estimates with their reproduced values. Reweighting is MSG finding being reproduced; no distinct alternative specifications testing MSG result are reported. New combined cohorts are independent extensions, not original-data robustness.""}"
10.1007/s00125-010-2002-7,47e3e03c3643c827,6,,"Genetic variant near IRS1 is associated with type 2 diabetes, insulin resistance and hyperinsulinemia",Rung,2009,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Rung"", ""year"": ""2009"", ""title"": ""Genetic variant near IRS1 is associated with type 2 diabetes, insulin resistance and hyperinsulinemia"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The MAGIC SNPs were supplemented by 13 additional SNPs from previously published GWASs (ESM Table 2) for a total of 29 SNPs that we attempted to replicate in our African-American cohort.\nRung\t40062.0\tNat Genet\tGenetic variant near IRS1 is associated with type 2 diabetes, insulin resistance and hyperinsulinemia\tType 2 diabetes and other traits"", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Using the local (LD-based) replication strategy, we replicated additional SNPs in or near previously reported genes, including the insulin receptor substrate 1 gene."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Mapped the study to its SNPs using primary ESM Table 2. Outcomes aggregate direct and local tests. For targets without individual narrative, the residual nonreplication statement supports failure with lower confidence; the local replication list refers to different discovery SNPs. Citation metadata limited to what this article and ESM print.""}"
10.1109/iwsc55060.2022.00015,a0f5200ab76450d1,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""In this paper, we aim to replicate and evaluate the performance of CodeBERT for code clone detection on multiple datasets with varying functionalities to understand (1) whether CodeBERT can generalize to unseen code, (2) how fine-tuning can effect CodeBERT’s performance on unseen code, and (3) how CodeBERT performs for detecting various code clone types."", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""outcome_quote"": """", ""outcome_quote_section"": """", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Abstract does not identify the target paper or state its original finding; reported performance cannot establish agreement.""}"
10.1017/s003329170500543x,bfd9f32714e4aa69,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Overall findings of our first direct interview family study of obsessive–compulsive disorder (OCD) indicated that OCD is familial. In this replication study, we carefully examined the role of informant data in ascertaining OCD in relatives."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""When relative diagnoses were derived without the benefit of proband informant reports, no evidence of familial OCD transmission was found. When diagnoses were made including information from the proband about the relative, evidence of familial OCD was found, but only when the diagnostic threshold was lowered to include cases with probable OCD or OCD symptoms."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Original familial association is stated. Authors report no familial evidence under one diagnostic procedure and evidence under another with a lowered threshold, and explicitly describe their results as inconsistent. Less robust support does not itself cause the mixed verdict; the contrasted presence and absence of the finding does.""}"
10.1080/17470210903377380,55cf730a319892f9,1,10.1037/0278-7393.30.3.723,The time course of recovery for grammatical class information during lexical processing for syntactic construction,"Pechmann, T.; Garrett, M. F.; Zerbst, D.",2004,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Pechmann, T.; Garrett, M. F.; Zerbst, D."", ""year"": ""2004"", ""title"": ""The time course of recovery for grammatical class information during lexical processing for syntactic construction"", ""doi"": ""10.1037/0278-7393.30.3.723"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""If the word class effect observed here and by Pechmann and colleagues (2004 ; Pechmann & Zerbst, 2002 ) does not reflect a lexical effect, it would remove such evidence as support for models that assume word class constraints on lexical selection (e.g., Dell et al., 2008 ). In Experiment 2, we tried to establish whether a word class effect could be obtained when the variable imageability was controlled."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Experiment 2 revealed that the word class effect could not be separated from the imageability effect: Naming latencies were sensitive to distractor imageability (see Lupker, 1979 ), but were not sensitive to distractor word class when imageability was controlled. These results conflict with the results of Pechmann and colleagues (2004 ; Pechmann & Zerbst, 2002 )."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors explicitly compare the tested word class effects to this original; outcome follows the reported agreement and disagreement.""}"
10.1371/journal.pone.0193787,ff87921f978478fb,0,,Implicit and explicit behavioral tendencies in male and female depression.,"Seidel E-M, Habel U, Finkelmeyer A, Schneider F, Gur RC, Derntl B.",2010,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Seidel E-M, Habel U, Finkelmeyer A, Schneider F, Gur RC, Derntl B."", ""year"": ""2010"", ""title"": ""Implicit and explicit behavioral tendencies in male and female depression."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Although we do not have previous genetic research to base our hypotheses on, based on clinical experiences, information processing theories in depression and the research by Seidel and colleagues [ 42 ], we expected avoidance of negative (sad, angry) facial expressions in remitted depressed S/L g -carriers who have experienced childhood trauma, as this represents the most vulnerable subgroup."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In line with Seidel and colleagues [ 42 ], we expected automatic avoidance of angry faces as well. We did not find this, however."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The specific re-tested Seidel finding is automatic avoidance of angry faces, explicitly not found. New sad-face/genetic interaction results are not a successful replication of Seidel’s angry-face effect.""}"
10.3389/fmicb.2018.01598,93b6eb882efbcfbb,1,10.1016/j.jpedsurg.2014.01.060,Intestinal dysbiosis and bacterial enteroinvasion in a murine model of Hirschsprung’s disease.,"Pierre, J. F., Barlow-Anacker, A. J., Erickson, C. S., Heneghan, A. F., Leverson, G. E., Dowd, S. E.",2014,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Pierre, J. F., Barlow-Anacker, A. J., Erickson, C. S., Heneghan, A. F., Leverson, G. E., Dowd, S. E."", ""year"": ""2014"", ""title"": ""Intestinal dysbiosis and bacterial enteroinvasion in a murine model of Hirschsprung’s disease."", ""doi"": ""10.1016/j.jpedsurg.2014.01.060"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Previous work using murine models of HSCR and HAEC revealed intestinal dysbiosis associated with aganglionosis, indicating a potential role for intestinal microbiomes in the promotion of HAEC (\nWard et al., 2012\n;\nPierre et al., 2014\n). We examined the reproducibility of these findings and also investigated the contribution of inter-facility effects to microbiome variability in C57BL/6J mice, reproducing the findings of\nRausch et al. (2016)\non a smaller scale."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Instead, Laramie Ednrb mice showed an enrichment of family Enterobacteriaceae from phylum Proteobacteria. Observations for these taxa are consistent with previously published work in another HSCR/HAEC mouse model (\nPierre et al., 2014\n) and shifts in Bacteroidetes (\nFrank et al., 2007\n;\nWalker et al., 2011\n) and Proteobacteria (\nFrank et al., 2007\n) have been reported in other IBD related to HAEC."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Introduction explicitly includes this paper among the dysbiosis findings re-tested; discussion compares concordant taxa.""}"
10.3389/fmicb.2018.01598,93b6eb882efbcfbb,2,10.1016/j.ijmm.2016.03.004,Analysis of factors contributing to variation in the c57bl/6j fecal microbiota across german animal facilities.,"Rausch, P., Basic, M., Batra, A., Bischoff, S. C., Blaut, M., Clavel, T.",2016,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Rausch, P., Basic, M., Batra, A., Bischoff, S. C., Blaut, M., Clavel, T."", ""year"": ""2016"", ""title"": ""Analysis of factors contributing to variation in the c57bl/6j fecal microbiota across german animal facilities."", ""doi"": ""10.1016/j.ijmm.2016.03.004"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Previous work using murine models of HSCR and HAEC revealed intestinal dysbiosis associated with aganglionosis, indicating a potential role for intestinal microbiomes in the promotion of HAEC (\nWard et al., 2012\n;\nPierre et al., 2014\n). We examined the reproducibility of these findings and also investigated the contribution of inter-facility effects to microbiome variability in C57BL/6J mice, reproducing the findings of\nRausch et al. (2016)\non a smaller scale."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The same facility-specific effect was also seen in fecal samples from C57BL/6J mice. These effects were independent of sex or cage influences. Our findings in C57BL/6J mice are consistent with a previous report (\nRausch et al., 2016\n)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Specific facility-effect replication explicitly stated and confirmed.""}"
