10.1287/orsc.2016.1065,a065b4f4a5188b65,1,,"Entrepreneurial spawning: Public corporations and the genesis of new ventures, 1986 to 1999.","Gompers P, Lerner J, Scharfstein DS",2005,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Gompers P, Lerner J, Scharfstein DS"", ""year"": ""2005"", ""title"": ""Entrepreneurial spawning: Public corporations and the genesis of new ventures, 1986 to 1999."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We begin by replicating prior results regarding firm size and the likelihood of becoming an entrepreneur (e.g., Elfenbein et al. 2010, Gompers et al. 2005, Kacperczyk 2012, Sørensen 2007)."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Overall, we are able to replicate the commonly found “small-firm effect,” but when we restrict our analyses to observe workers in dissolved firms alone, we no longer find the negative effect."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors confirm the cited small-firm result in the ordinary sample and explicitly report its disappearance and reversal among defunct firms. Their aggregate statement distinguishes these outcomes.""}"
