he was positively correlated with activity in the VLPFC (r = -0.77, prep = 0.99)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Individual primary replication report: authors’ verdict and comparisons to this original, prioritizing an overall verdict when stated.""}"
10.1017/jdm.2024.31,6dfd87d4b0761f76,1,10.1016/j.obhdp.2007.04.006,"""One of us"": Outstanding willingness to help save a single identified compatriot","Kogut, T., & Ritov, I.",2007,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Kogut, T., & Ritov, I."", ""year"": ""2007"", ""title"": ""\""One of us\"": Outstanding willingness to help save a single identified compatriot"", ""doi"": ""10.1016/j.obhdp.2007.04.006"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We sought to revisit this classic finding and re-examine the conditions which encourage or discourage helping intention. We report an independent close replication of the identified single victim effect demonstrated in Kogut and Ritov (2005a) Study 2 along with two extensions, testing the effect of group belonging as a conceptual replication of Kogut and Ritov (2007) and examining perceived responsibility (Erlandsson et al., 2015)."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In our extension, we failed to find support for the findings by Kogut and Ritov (2007) that there is a higher likelihood of helping single victims over a group if they belong to the ingroup (vs. the outgroup)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Explicit author verdict on the target effect.""}"
10.1525/collabra.310,f641e4b5cb3f280b,2,10.1016/j.jbusres.2019.01.027,"The 1 in 1,000,000: Context effects of how numbers cue different kinds of incidental environmental anchoring in marketing communications.","Koçaş, C., & Dogerlioglu-Demir, K.",2020,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Koçaş, C., & Dogerlioglu-Demir, K."", ""year"": ""2020"", ""title"": ""The 1 in 1,000,000: Context effects of how numbers cue different kinds of incidental environmental anchoring in marketing communications."", ""doi"": ""10.1016/j.jbusres.2019.01.027"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2, 3"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Thus our procedure represents a close (near exact in Study 3) replication of the equivalent studies by Critcher and Gilovich (2008), Dogerlioglu-Demir and Koçaş (2015), and Koçaş and Dogerlioglu-Demir (2020) but nevertheless failed to detect an incidental anchoring effect."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Thus our procedure represents a close (near exact in Study 3) replication of the equivalent studies by Critcher and Gilovich (2008), Dogerlioglu-Demir and Koçaş (2015), and Koçaş and Dogerlioglu-Demir (2020) but nevertheless failed to detect an incidental anchoring effect."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true}"
10.1525/collabra.310,f641e4b5cb3f280b,3,10.1126/science.185.4157.1124,Judgment under uncertainty: Heuristics and biases.,"Tversky, A., & Kahneman, D.",1974,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Tversky, A., & Kahneman, D."", ""year"": ""1974"", ""title"": ""Judgment under uncertainty: Heuristics and biases."", ""doi"": ""10.1126/science.185.4157.1124"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""2, 3"", ""evidence_quote"": ""These items enabled a replication of the classic anchoring procedure designed by Tversky and Kahneman (1974)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In contrast to the incidental anchoring results, for these standard items robust anchoring effects were obtained (p < .001 in each case)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_confident"": true}"
10.1007/s40732-024-00597-x,7137b2b0d9b69364,0,10.1002/jeab.727,Effects of immediate-reinforcement training on delay discounting behavior in rats,"Fox, A. E.",2022,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Fox, A. E."", ""year"": ""2022"", ""title"": ""Effects of immediate-reinforcement training on delay discounting behavior in rats"", ""doi"": ""10.1002/jeab.727"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Experimental work investigating possible causal rela-\n\ntions between impulsive choice and extended experience\n\nwith immediate reinforcement is limited, but some work\n\nsuggests that experience with immediate reinforcement\n\nmay subsequently increase impulsive choice or blunt matu-\n\nrational decreases in impulsive choice in a delay-discounting\nparadigm (Fox, 2022; Fox etal., 2019; Renda etal., 2018;\n\nSmith etal., 2022). It was the goal of the work presented\n\nhere to expand on this body of research by testing eﬀects\n\nof an immediate reinforcement intervention that was 50%\n\nlonger in duration, included approximately twice as many\n\nimmediate reinforcement training trials, and employed twice\n\nthe sample, than previous work. In doing so, we hoped to\n\ntest the limits and generality of previously reported results."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""A group of rats was exposed to an immediate (no-delay)-\n\nreinforcement intervention and subsequent delay discounting\n\nbehavior was modestly, but statistically signiﬁcantly, more\n\nimpulsive compared to a control group. This is consistent\n\nwith previous work (Fox, 2022; Fox etal., 2019; Renda\n\netal., 2018; Smith etal., 2022), and with the conclusion\nthat prolonged exposure to immediate reinforcement may\n\nhave just modest impacts on subsequent impulsive choice,\n\ncompared to relatively larger impacts of exposure to delayed\n\nreinforcement. This was true in the present experiment"", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The introduction identifies the set of prior immediate-reinforcement findings whose generality is tested; the discussion explicitly reports consistency with every named member of that set. Modest magnitude is not framed as failure.""}"
