10.1016/j.xjmad.2024.100075,cc4593dd95b8c649,1,,Attention bias to negative versus non-negative faces is related to negative affectivity in a transdiagnostic youth sample.,"Harrewijn, A., Abend, R., Naim, R., Haller, S. P., Stavish, C. M., Bajaj, M. A., et al.",2021,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Harrewijn, A., Abend, R., Naim, R., Haller, S. P., Stavish, C. M., Bajaj, M. A., et al."", ""year"": ""2021"", ""title"": ""Attention bias to negative versus non-negative faces is related to negative affectivity in a transdiagnostic youth sample."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Second, to replicate and extend previous findings of Harrewijn et al. [15], we hypothesized that attention bias towards negative-valence face stimuli as measured with eye-tracking would be associated with facets of negative affectivity."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Second, hypotheses that previous findings of Abend et al. [14] would be replicated were not supported, nor were hypotheses that previous findings of Harrewijn et al. [15] would be replicated."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Explicit overall verdict names this original as not replicated. Additional narrow-band associations and cautions about sample size do not override that verdict.""}"
10.1007/s11002-025-09771-9,c7f5a25a2191611b,1,10.1093/jcr/ucy061,Does time of day affect variety-seeking?,"Gullo, K., Berger, J., Etkin, J., & Bollinger, B.",2019,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Gullo, K., Berger, J., Etkin, J., & Bollinger, B."", ""year"": ""2019"", ""title"": ""Does time of day affect variety-seeking?"", ""doi"": ""10.1093/jcr/ucy061"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We propose a replication on the effect of the time of day on variety-seeking behavior, given the results from the two papers were different and there were some limitations of the previous research. For example, Roehm and Roehm (\n2004\n) used small samples of students and one product category (candy bars) that limits generalizability while Gullo et al. (\n2019\n) used households in their first study that could mask individual behavior."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Similar to Gullo et al. (2019), we found variety-seeking increases from the morning to the afternoon. Additionally, we found a post-lunch dip. Our results as such lend further support to the matching mechanism between variety-seeking and diurnal level proposed by Gullo et al. (2019) rather than the compensating mechanism suggested by Roehm and Roehm (2004)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The new UK sample confirms the increasing pattern reported by Gullo and contradicts the decreasing pattern reported by Roehm; the authors explicitly favor the matching mechanism.""}"
10.1080/23273798.2016.1197954,3eb87368cec5640c,2,10.1037/xlm0000013,Preview benefit in English spaced compounds.,"Cutter, M. G., Drieghe, D., & Liversedge, S. P.",2014,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Cutter, M. G., Drieghe, D., & Liversedge, S. P."", ""year"": ""2014"", ""title"": ""Preview benefit in English spaced compounds."", ""doi"": ""10.1037/xlm0000013"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""In the current study we investigated whether a similar phenomenon occurs in Chinese reading; namely, that when word n+2 forms a multi-word unit with word n+1, parafoveal processing of word n+2 may occur to a larger extent than previously observed."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""It is worth considering the characteristics of our idioms which may have led to them not being processed as multi-word units in the parafovea, in contrast to the spaced compounds investigated by Cutter et al. (2014)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors explicitly compare the tested finding with this original.""}"
