10.1177/1948550616683017,ff70bf5ad942cddd,1,10.3102/0002831211410683,Implicit social cognitions predict sex differences in math engagement and achievement.,"Nosek, B. A., & Smyth, F. L.",2011,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Nosek, B. A., & Smyth, F. L."", ""year"": ""2011"", ""title"": ""Implicit social cognitions predict sex differences in math engagement and achievement."", ""doi"": ""10.3102/0002831211410683"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Nosek and Smyth (2011) compared the gender-science IAT and the explicit stereotype (measured with two self-reported items: Men are better at math than women are and Women can achieve as much as men in math) in predicting math-related outcomes. We re-analyzed that data and found only one variable, the difference between math and verbal SAT scores, that had reliably stronger relation with the IAT (r = .193, p < .001) than the explicit stereotype (r = .090, p = .004), Williams' t(1040) = 2.590, p = .010."", ""outcome"": """", ""outcome_quote"": """", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": ""not checked"", ""outcome_computational_quote"": ""To refute that possibility, we returned to previous research that found an advantage of the IAT, and tested whether the evidence for this advantage persists even when controlling for self-reported liking."", ""outcome_robustness"": ""robustness challenges"", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": ""Importantly, when we computed partial correlations between each measure and the SAT, partialling out shared variance with attitudes, there was no longer reliable evidence for an IAT advantage: The IAT/SAT relation (r =.049, p = .115) was not significantly better than the explicit stereotype/SAT relation (r = .017, p = .584), Williams' t(1040) < 1, p = .442."", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Analysis directly tests a covariate-adjusted specification; it does not report an audit of recovery of original numerical results. The IAT advantage disappears with attitude adjustment.""}"
10.1037/xap0000366,257c330f3972ab87,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,"{""corrected_value"": null, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""257c330f3972ab87"", ""location"": ""Abstract (line 6)"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""Reassigning responsibility is the most prominent and best-replicated intervention against escalating commitment (i.e., the failure to withdraw from losing courses of action). This intervention is considered effective because it reduces reinvestments after negative feedback in decision scenarios with a single reinvestment decision. However, we argue that any intervention against escalating commitment should fulfill two additional criteria. The first is temporal stability, that is, the beneficial effects of the intervention need to persist beyond a single reinvestment decision. The second is specific effectiveness, that is, the intervention should reduce commitment only if the project continues to fail after an initial setback (structural failure) but not if it recovers and is ultimately profitable (temporary failure). To subject reassignment of responsibility to this critical test of effectiveness, we introduce a modification of the escalation paradigm that allows testing for temporal stability and differentiates between structural and temporary failure."", ""reason"": ""The available source text (10.1037/xap0000366, Schultze & Schulz-Hardt, 2022) consists only of the title, authors, DOI, and abstract. The abstract states that the paper empirically retests the intervention of reassigning responsibility against escalating commitment across two experiments using a modified escalation paradigm. However, the abstract contains no citations, author names, publication years, titles, or DOIs for the original study or studies establishing the responsibility-reassignment intervention or escalation paradigm. Consequently, primary source evidence is insufficient to establish whether the retested original is already represented by shipped row 1509 (Staw, 1976, 'Knee-deep in the big muddy: a study of escalating commitment to a chosen course of action', DOI 10.1016/0030-5073(76)90005-2) or whether an unshipped original paper is being retested."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0012_257c330f3972ab87"", ""target_index"": 0, ""verdict"": ""undeterminable""}","{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""To subject reassignment of responsibility to this critical test of effectiveness, we introduce a modification of the escalation paradigm that allows testing for temporal stability and differentiates between structural and temporary failure."", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""outcome_quote"": """", ""outcome_quote_section"": """", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Abstract reports challenges to temporal stability and specificity, but does not identify any original paper or assign comparisons to particular originals. Cannot attribute an outcome to an identifiable target. One blank entry denotes an unidentified target relationship, not a verified count of originals.""}"
10.1080/20445911.2020.1805453,ed701519c48a420b,1,10.1080/20445911.2019.1659801,Anticipation-speciﬁc reliability and trial-to-trial carryover of anticipatory attentional bias for threat.,"Gladwin, T. E., Figner, B., & Vink, M.",2019,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Gladwin, T. E., Figner, B., & Vink, M."", ""year"": ""2019"", ""title"": ""Anticipation-speciﬁc reliability and trial-to-trial carryover of anticipatory attentional bias for threat."", ""doi"": ""10.1080/20445911.2019.1659801"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""The predicted within-subject eﬀects were found.\nFor Colour cues, attentional bias was drawn\ntowards the colour cue on which the target was presented in the previous trial. This replicated the previous ﬁnding on colour cues and carryover\n(Gladwin & Figner, 2019). Carryover was also found\nfor negative facial expressions, as in previous\nﬁndings for threatening stimuli (Gladwin et al.,\n2019; Gladwin & Figner, 2019)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The predicted within-subject eﬀects were found.\nFor Colour cues, attentional bias was drawn\ntowards the colour cue on which the target was presented in the previous trial. This replicated the previous ﬁnding on colour cues and carryover\n(Gladwin & Figner, 2019). Carryover was also found\nfor negative facial expressions, as in previous\nﬁndings for threatening stimuli (Gladwin et al.,\n2019; Gladwin & Figner, 2019)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Both cited findings are explicitly compared as confirmed by new samples and stimulus sets. Low individual-difference reliability does not invalidate the within-subject carryover finding; Study 2 also reports its replication.""}"
