10.1080/1612197x.2016.1199580,9f0083cada5fd3bb,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Recent research on exercise with virtually present partners found that encouragement attenuated the Köhler effect, which boosts task motivation for weaker group members in conjunctive tasks. The current study replicated and extended previous research, comparing exclusive (e.g. “you can do it”) and inclusive encouragement (e.g. “we can do it”) as potential moderators of the Köhler effect."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""A significant motivation gain was observed in all partnered conditions compared to the control, and exclusive encouragement from a virtually present partner attenuated motivation gains. However, inclusive encouragement did not, and encouragement from an audible trainer boosted exercise motivation over the control group."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Abstract states the previous finding that encouragement attenuated motivation gains and reports the corresponding attenuation under exclusive encouragement. Inclusive encouragement and audible trainer are extensions; no explicit contrast with the original is stated.""}"
10.2466/20.22.pms.114.1.185-188,29cbe0ff7f624a59,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,"{""corrected_value"": null, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""29cbe0ff7f624a59"", ""location"": ""Abstract (line 5); see also Title (line 1)"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""One of the early studies supporting his model was conducted by Victorina Lefebvre. Over the years, other studies have been conducted, but not all have shown similar support as the original findings of Victorina Lefebvre. This study attempted to extend her findings in testing whether or not other ambiguous stimuli could be used in support of the model."", ""reason"": ""No prior adjudication or state file exists for this work. The supplied primary source (29cbe0ff7f624a59.txt) contains only the title ('Asymmetry in Human Cognition: Further Replication and Extension'), author list, and a five-sentence abstract, with no full text or reference list. Contrary to Luna's triage disposal as not_a_target, unseen full text cannot be used to exclude a paper whose title explicitly describes it as a 'Further Replication and Extension'. However, because only the abstract is available, all citation fields in the blind target are empty and the evidence cannot resolve the precise questions of: (1) whether the full text empirically retests a specific published finding in addition to testing new ambiguous stimuli, (2) what the source-backed bibliographic citation is for Victorina Lefebvre's study, and (3) whether under the codebook's replication-chain rule the retested target is already represented by shipped row 2121 (Anderson & Grice, 2009, 'Replicating a Simple Study of Asymmetry in Human Cognition', Perceptual and Motor Skills, 109(2), 577-580, doi_o: 10.2466/pms.109.2.577-580, by the same first author James A. Anderson) or is an unshipped work by Victorina Lefebvre."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0018_29cbe0ff7f624a59"", ""target_index"": 0, ""verdict"": ""undeterminable""}","{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""This study attempted to extend her findings in testing whether or not other ambiguous stimuli could be used in support of the model."", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""outcome_quote"": """", ""outcome_quote_section"": """", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The abstract identifies an attempt to extend Victorina Lefebvre’s findings but supplies neither a unique bibliographic entry nor results. No target citation is guessed from background references.""}"
10.1016/j.jecp.2015.12.009,eb5d4130a63b4193,0,,,Borst et al.,2015,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Borst et al."", ""year"": ""2015"", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""A previous study demonstrated that preventing mirror errors in reading requires the inhibition of the mirror generalization process in expert adult readers (Borst et al., 2015). Using the same experimental paradigm, the current study aimed at replicating this result in school-aged children."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Crucially, children required more time to determine that two animals facing opposite directions were identical when preceded by two letters that were lateral mirror images of one another (b/d) than when preceded by letters that were not mirror images of one another (f/t). Importantly, the amplitude of the negative priming effect did not vary with age. Our results suggest that overcoming mirror errors in reading, regardless of the reading proficiency of school-aged children, is rooted in the ability to inhibit the mirror generalization process."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The abstract states the original inhibitory mechanism and reports the corresponding negative priming result in the new sample. The proposed age effect is an extension rather than a contradicted original result.""}"
10.3389/fpsyg.2016.01930,1afa9693a775a922,1,10.3758/s13414-013-0430-4,Continuous hand movement induces a far-hand bias in attentional priority.,Festman Y. Adam J. J. Pratt J. Fischer M. H.,2013,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,"{""file_key"": ""1afa9693a775a922"", ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""target_index"": 1, ""method"": ""obvious"", ""verdict"": ""undeterminable"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""We observed evidence for a directionality effect in the spatial gap discrimination task, as reflected in enhanced performance when moving toward as compared to moving away from the probe, which provides a replication of the previously established directionality effects in spatial tasks ( Festman et al., 2013a , b )."", ""location"": ""Independent parent primary context and original-report chain, integration/original_report_chain_0000.result.json"", ""reason"": ""Current article jointly attributes one retested directionality finding to2013aand2013b; original2013bexplicitly repeats2013afar-hand effect in a new sample before its static-left-hand extension. The codebook selects closest actual retested chain finding. Confirm2013b separately from shippedGozli2012, but do not list2013a again merely because it has another DOI/new original experiment. Current right-hand-response/left-in-lap protocol resemblesa, while joint prediction citesb. This leaves an actual closest-chain versus separate-target ambiguity after full original inspection; keepa unresolved rather than affirming a second genuinely absent pair or forcing a background exclusion.""}","{""authors"": ""Festman Y. Adam J. J. Pratt J. Fischer M. H."", ""year"": ""2013"", ""title"": ""Continuous hand movement induces a far-hand bias in attentional priority."", ""doi"": ""10.3758/s13414-013-0430-4"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Furthermore, on the basis of the direction effect of Festman et al. (2013a , b ), we predicted better discrimination when moving toward the probe in both tasks."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In addition, we obtained (weak) statistical evidence for a directionality effect, that is, enhanced performance when moving toward compared to away from the probe, but only in spatial gap discrimination."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Directionality was predicted in both tasks, and authors explicitly report it only in spatial discrimination. They call spatial directionality a replication of both Festman papers; aggregate across the two experiments is mixed.""}"
10.1353/eca.2016.0000,2050e50097e7846c,0,,How Elastic Are Preferences for Redistribution? Evidence from Randomized Survey Experiments.,"Kuziemko, Ilyana, Michael I. Norton, Emmanuel Saez, and Stefanie Stantcheva.",2013,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Kuziemko, Ilyana, Michael I. Norton, Emmanuel Saez, and Stefanie Stantcheva."", ""year"": ""2013"", ""title"": ""How Elastic Are Preferences for Redistribution? Evidence from Randomized Survey Experiments."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""In reality, demand for\nincome redistribution in the United States has remained ﬂat by some mea-\nsures and decreased according to others (see Kuziemko and others 2013),\nas we document later in this paper.\n\nAcross the four measures, we are able\nto replicate the ﬁnding of previous literature showing no increase in sup-\nport for redistribution over this period of increased inequality."", ""outcome"": """", ""outcome_quote"": """", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": ""not checked"", ""outcome_computational_quote"": ""To ensure that our heterogeneous patterns\nare not driven by data or coding differences between our paper and previ-\nous work, we ﬁrst demonstrate that we can replicate the earlier ﬁnding of\nﬂat aggregate demand using our survey measures."", ""outcome_robustness"": ""robust"", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": ""Across the four measures, we are able\nto replicate the ﬁnding of previous literature showing no increase in sup-\nport for redistribution over this period of increased inequality."", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The earlier flat aggregate trend survives alternative measures and survey datasets. Exact original numerical estimates are not the object of the analysis.""}"
