10.3389/fpsyg.2016.00630,9ae87d1f87fc5885,1,10.1162/jocn.2008.20099,Involuntary attentional capture is determined by task set: evidence from event-related brain potentials.,"Eimer, M., Kiss, M.",2008,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Eimer, M., Kiss, M."", ""year"": ""2008"", ""title"": ""Involuntary attentional capture is determined by task set: evidence from event-related brain potentials."", ""doi"": ""10.1162/jocn.2008.20099"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Previous studies have shown that cues in the target color capture attention, whereas neutral cues do not ( Eimer and Kiss, 2008 ; Lien et al., 2008 ). We hope to replicate this result in the present study."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Instead, we observed cueing benefits and an N2pc to cues in the go-color, confirming that response-relevant features capture attention ( Eimer and Kiss, 2008 ; Lien et al., 2008 )."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true}"
10.3389/fpsyg.2016.00630,9ae87d1f87fc5885,2,10.1037/0096-1523.34.3.509,Contingent attentional capture by top-down control settings: converging evidence from event-related potentials.,"Lien, M.-C., Ruthruff, E., Goodin, Z., Remington, R. W.",2008,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Lien, M.-C., Ruthruff, E., Goodin, Z., Remington, R. W."", ""year"": ""2008"", ""title"": ""Contingent attentional capture by top-down control settings: converging evidence from event-related potentials."", ""doi"": ""10.1037/0096-1523.34.3.509"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Previous studies have shown that cues in the target color capture attention, whereas neutral cues do not ( Eimer and Kiss, 2008 ; Lien et al., 2008 ). We hope to replicate this result in the present study."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Instead, we observed cueing benefits and an N2pc to cues in the go-color, confirming that response-relevant features capture attention ( Eimer and Kiss, 2008 ; Lien et al., 2008 )."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true}"
10.3389/fpsyg.2016.00630,9ae87d1f87fc5885,3,10.1037/a0015872,The roles of feature-specific task set and bottom-up salience in attentional capture: an ERP study.,"Eimer, M., Kiss, M., Press, C., Sauter, D.",2009,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Eimer, M., Kiss, M., Press, C., Sauter, D."", ""year"": ""2009"", ""title"": ""The roles of feature-specific task set and bottom-up salience in attentional capture: an ERP study."", ""doi"": ""10.1037/a0015872"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Previously, Eimer et al. (2009) had observed an enhanced N2 component in response to color-cues that did not match the target cues. Thus, the most straightforward prediction is that an enhanced N2 occurs for neutral and nogo cues alike because both do not match the go-color."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Of importance, Eimer et al. (2009) showed that the negativity at anterior electrodes was larger with non-matching than matching cues, suggesting that inhibition occurred when cues did not match the top–down set. Similarly, we observed a larger negativity for non-matching nogo cues. However, the negativity was the same for neutral cues, which suggests that the N2 does not reflect inhibition of nogo-stimuli, but rather activation of response-relevant stimuli (see Bruin et al., 2001 )."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true}"
10.1177/21674795231219742,f65b8567ad2e765d,0,,,Anderson; Raney,2018,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Anderson; Raney"", ""year"": ""2018"", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Anderson and Raney (2018) explored this in an experimental study ( n = 234) and found evidence of the Black criminal stereotype among sports fans. However, more research was needed to further explore this phenomenon. The current study employed a similar experimental design ( n = 603) in an attempt to (1) replicate their 2018 study to examine whether sports fans’ perceptions of criminal athletes have changed over the past several years, especially in light of the Black Lives Matter movement, and (2) determine whether the sport played by an alleged criminal athlete might be linked to judgments regarding race and crime in sports."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Our findings indicate that sports fandom still predicts stereotypical judgments."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The abstract states the original found the Black criminal stereotype among sports fans and the replication finds fandom still predicts stereotypical judgments. Stronger alternative predictors do not contradict the stated replicated effect.""}"
10.20381/ruor-28430,d004173d837cb2ee,0,,Effectiveness of different adjunctive interventions in the management of orthodontically induced white spot lesions: A systematic review of systematic reviews and meta-analyses.,"Bakdach, W.M.M. and R. Hadad",2020,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Bakdach, W.M.M. and R. Hadad"", ""year"": ""2020"", ""title"": ""Effectiveness of different adjunctive interventions in the management of orthodontically induced white spot lesions: A systematic review of systematic reviews and meta-analyses."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We replicated the Jadad assessments and evaluated whether we chose the same SR, whether a Cochrane SR was chosen, and whether we followed the same steps as the Discordant Review authors."", ""outcome"": """", ""outcome_quote"": """", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": ""not checked"", ""outcome_computational_quote"": ""Information and data required to complete the Jadad algorithm were \u0000rst sought directly from the Discordant Reviews, and if not reported, the full texts of the included SRs."", ""outcome_robustness"": ""robustness challenges"", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": ""We replicated the Jadad assessments and evaluated whether we chose the same SR, whether a Cochrane SR was chosen, and whether we followed the same steps as the Discordant Review authors."", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Table 2 reports different selected systematic review(s) under independent reassessment; this materially changes the central selection judgment even when clinical direction agrees. Original numerical estimates were extracted rather than rerun. The central check is sensitivity of review selection to independent interpretation of Jadad rules. Table entries, rather than a target-specific sentence, settle the robustness axis."", ""reference_number"": 13}"
