10.31577/sp.2021.03.824,d5d12db97a6155d5,5,10.1007/s10683-015-9457-9,A theoretical and experimental appraisal of four risk elicitation methods.,"Crosetto, P., & Filippin, A.",2016,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Crosetto, P., & Filippin, A."", ""year"": ""2016"", ""title"": ""A theoretical and experimental appraisal of four risk elicitation methods."", ""doi"": ""10.1007/s10683-015-9457-9"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Previous research showed evidence that risk preferences elicited with different measures may significantly differ and that the measures provide results that are not robust across the contexts (Crosetto & Filippin, 2016; Csermely & Rabas, 2016; Pedroni et al., 2017). In line with these findings, we explored the issue of risk measures consistency in our study. We found that the three risk elicitation methods did not correlate or correlated weakly."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Previous research showed evidence that risk preferences elicited with different measures may significantly differ and that the measures provide results that are not robust across the contexts (Crosetto & Filippin, 2016; Csermely & Rabas, 2016; Pedroni et al., 2017). In line with these findings, we explored the issue of risk measures consistency in our study. We found that the three risk elicitation methods did not correlate or correlated weakly."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors directly compare the tested finding with this cited original.""}"
