10.1046/j.1365-2702.1999.00307.x,26e3db6b12b038fe,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,"{""corrected_value"": null, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""file_key"": ""26e3db6b12b038fe"", ""location"": ""Abstract (line 4)"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""quote"": ""• The findings of a single small‐scale study previously indicated that such patients’ needs for information are not always fully met and pain control is inadequate.• This small‐scale study was replicated to produce broadly similar findings in a second hospital 2 years later."", ""reason"": ""The available source text (Fox, Gould, Davies, & Owen, 1999, 'Patients’ experiences of having an underwater seal chest drain: a replication study') contains only the title, author list, and abstract. While the abstract explicitly states that a previous single small-scale study on chest drain patients' information needs and pain control was empirically replicated in a second hospital two years later with broadly similar findings, the abstract gives no author names, year, title, or DOI for that prior study. Without full text or references in the primary source, it cannot be verified from source evidence alone whether the replicated study is the shipped row 2433 (Owen & Gould, 1997, 'Underwater seal chest drains: the patient's experience', DOI 10.1111/j.1365-2702.1997.tb00307.x) or a different unshipped report."", ""request_id"": ""luna_0012_26e3db6b12b038fe"", ""target_index"": 0, ""verdict"": ""undeterminable""}","{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The findings of a single small‐scale study previously indicated that such patients’ needs for information are not always fully met and pain control is inadequate. This small‐scale study was replicated to produce broadly similar findings in a second hospital 2 years later."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""This small‐scale study was replicated to produce broadly similar findings in a second hospital 2 years later."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The abstract gives an overall verdict of broadly similar findings.""}"
10.1017/s1352465810000676,9b05053aa65fde14,1,,"The relationship between disgust sensitivity, anxiety and obsessions.","Thorpe, S. J., Patel, S. P. and Simonds, L. M.",2003,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Thorpe, S. J., Patel, S. P. and Simonds, L. M."", ""year"": ""2003"", ""title"": ""The relationship between disgust sensitivity, anxiety and obsessions."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""This paper therefore sets out to explore the posited relationship between disgust sensitivity,\nhealth anxiety, danger expectancies and cognitive and behavioural measures of hand washing,\nin vivo, within a non-clinical group. It builds specifically on the work by Jones and Menzies\n(1997) and Thorpe et al. (2003) and will do this by replicating the experimental methodology\ndescribed by Jones and Menzies (1997), by removing the elements that they found to\nbe redundant (responsibility and perfectionism), retaining those factors related to danger\nexpectancies concerning illness beliefs, and adding factors previously identified as being\nimplicated in self-reported levels of obsessional hand washing: namely, measures of self-\nreported disgust sensitivity and health anxiety (Thorpe et al., 2003)."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Health anxiety, for example, was not related to washing time but was related to the\nOCI-R washing subscale. However, our analysis showed that disgust sensitivity mediated the\nrelationship between health anxiety and OCI-R washing. This is different to previous findings\nby Thorpe et al. (2003) and may be due to the BAT heightening disgust sensitivity or other\n\f                        Disgust, danger expectancy and hand washing                           187\n\n\nsituational factors."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Named foundational study is retested with questionnaire and behavioural outcomes; health anxiety and disgust associations persist but the authors explicitly identify a different mediation pattern.""}"
10.1080/15248372.2015.1033525,a94c4cdacbb28b3b,1,,Representational change and children’s numerical estimation.,"Opfer, J. E., & Siegler, R. S.",2007,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Opfer, J. E., & Siegler, R. S."", ""year"": ""2007"", ""title"": ""Representational change and children’s numerical estimation."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""First, we wanted to directly replicate Siegler’s work (Opfer & Siegler, 2007)as closely as possible and thus chose to use the original positive number line and values used in those studies."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Our findings replicate those of Siegler and colleagues (e.g., Booth & Siegler, 2006) for positive numbers; specifically, we found evidence of a shift from logarithmic to linear representations for positive numbers between fourth and sixth grade (0–1,000 scale)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors state an overall replication verdict for the positive-number findings of Siegler and colleagues. Both cited papers are explicitly identified as work this study was designed to replicate.""}"
10.1002/1520-6629(199110)19:4<373::aid-jcop2290190409>3.0.co;2-j,6d95c6ae74f8dc7b,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The present study tested the following hypotheses: (1) Stress has more of a negative impact on the life satisfaction of those under age 75 than those over age 75; (2) social support has more of a positive effect on life satisfaction in those under age 75 than those over age 75; (3) the buffering effect of social support is stronger in the under age 75 group than the over age 75 group. None of the three hypotheses was supported, contrary to prior research."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The present study tested the following hypotheses: (1) Stress has more of a negative impact on the life satisfaction of those under age 75 than those over age 75; (2) social support has more of a positive effect on life satisfaction in those under age 75 than those over age 75; (3) the buffering effect of social support is stronger in the under age 75 group than the over age 75 group. None of the three hypotheses was supported, contrary to prior research."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The abstract explicitly says none of the three tested age-effect hypotheses was supported, contrary to prior research; title explicitly frames failure to replicate.""}"
